"""SEO wave 2026-07-24: 14 pages targeting forum-only SERPs, drafted + adversarially
verified (all 16 MUST fixes applied). Rendered by build_extra.build_new_page()."""

SEO_WAVE_PAGES = [{'slug': 'printer-wont-connect-to-mesh-wifi', 'title': "Printer Won't Connect to Mesh WiFi? Fixes | 365 Techies", 'metaDesc': "Printer won't join your mesh WiFi? Band steering, changing IPs and client isolation are usually to blame. Plain-English fixes from 365 Techies, Bournemouth.", 'ogTitle': "Printer Won't Connect to Mesh WiFi? The Real Reasons — and the Fixes", 'crumbName': 'Printer vs Mesh WiFi', 'eyebrow': '// MESH WIFI TROUBLESHOOTING', 'h1': "Printer Won't Connect to Mesh WiFi? Here's What's Actually Going On", 'lede': "Your printer worked for years on the old router, then you upgraded to mesh and it vanished. You're not imagining it — mesh systems do a few clever things (one network name, band steering, roaming between nodes) that printers genuinely hate. The good news: nearly every case comes down to three real causes plus one impostor — plain weak signal, which mimics all three — and every one has a proper fix. Here they are, in plain English.", 'chips': ['No fix, no fee', 'Rated 4.9 on Google', 'Trading since 1995'], 'ctaHead': 'Still fighting the printer?', 'ctaSub': "We sort mesh-and-printer standoffs remotely most days — no fix, no fee. Call 01202 775566 and we'll take a look together.", 'serviceName': 'Printer and Mesh WiFi Support', 'sections': [{'eyebrow': '/01 &mdash; THE SYMPTOM', 'h2': 'New mesh, vanished printer', 'html': '<p>You\'ve upgraded to a mesh WiFi system — and your printer, which behaved for years on the old router, now refuses to join. Or it joins, prints once, then shows as offline on every computer in the house. Sometimes the printer\'s own setup screen can\'t even find your new network. This is one of the most common mesh complaints we hear, and it\'s rarely the printer\'s fault alone.</p><p>Mesh systems do four things differently from a traditional router — one network name shared across two radio bands, automatic band steering, roaming between nodes, and (on some systems) device isolation — and each one can trip up a printer. This page walks through the three causes and their fixes — plus the impostor, weak signal, that mimics all of them, then finishes with the honest bit: when to stop fighting the WiFi altogether.</p><p>One boundary first: this page is about mesh-specific failures. If your printer drops out on any router, not just mesh, that\'s a different problem — see <a href="/why-does-my-printer-keep-disconnecting/">why does my printer keep disconnecting</a> instead.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/02 &mdash; THE CAUSES', 'h2': 'Four things mesh does that printers hate', 'html': '<p>Most home printers — even fairly recent ones — only have a 2.4GHz radio. A traditional router usually broadcasts 2.4GHz and 5GHz as two separately named networks, so you simply picked the 2.4 one. Mesh systems typically merge both bands under a single name and use band steering to decide, device by device, which band you get. Phones cope fine. Printers, with their cheap and often elderly WiFi chips, sometimes fumble the negotiation — they can see the network but fail to join, or fail silently partway through setup.</p><p>Second: addresses. When a printer drops off and rejoins — which happens more on mesh, as devices shuffle between nodes — it starts a fresh DHCP conversation and can come back on a different IP address. Your computer\'s printer driver still points at the old one, so the printer shows offline even though it\'s connected.</p><p>Third: some systems, particularly on guest or IoT networks, switch on client isolation — devices can reach the internet but can\'t see each other. A printer on an isolated network is invisible to every computer in the house.</p><p>Different brands handle all of this differently; our <a href="/mesh-wifi-systems-uk/">mesh WiFi systems guide</a> covers who does what.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/03 &mdash; GETTING CONNECTED', 'h2': 'Get the printer a clean 2.4GHz connection', 'html': '<p>Start with the band problem, because it blocks everything else. Open your mesh app and look for a setting that gives you a separate 2.4GHz network — brands call it an IoT network, a smart-home network, or simply a 2.4GHz SSID, and the names move between app versions, so hunt for the idea rather than an exact menu path. If your system offers one, join the printer to it and you\'ve sidestepped band steering entirely.</p><p>No way to split the bands? Two honest workarounds. Some systems can temporarily pause the 5GHz band for a few minutes while a stubborn device joins — check your app\'s settings or the maker\'s help pages. And because many printer setup apps copy the WiFi connection your phone is currently using, it helps if the phone is on 2.4GHz at that moment; 2.4GHz carries further than 5GHz, so stepping well away from the nearest node makes the phone more likely to fall back to it. Clunky, but it works surprisingly often.</p><p>If you\'re setting the whole system up fresh, doing things in the right order avoids most of this pain — our <a href="/mesh-wifi-setup-guide/">mesh WiFi setup guide</a> covers the sequence.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/04 &mdash; STAYING CONNECTED', 'h2': 'Pin the address so it stays found', 'html': '<p>Once the printer connects, two settings stop the weekly “offline” drama. First, give it a fixed address: in your mesh app, look for DHCP reservation (sometimes called a reserved or static IP) and pin the printer to one address. Second, on each computer, add the printer by its IP address rather than letting Windows or macOS discover it by name — the add-printer screens on both accept an IP directly, and the standard connection types (IPP, or the raw print port some drivers list as 9100) work fine over mesh. With a reserved address, the driver never chases a moving target again.</p><p>Also check isolation. If the printer sits on a guest network, move it — guest networks commonly isolate devices from each other by design. If your system has a client, device or AP isolation toggle, make sure it\'s off for the network the printer lives on.</p><p>Finally, rule out plain weak signal, which mimics all of the above. A printer in a far corner behind a sofa struggles regardless of settings — run our free <a href="/wifi-signal-test/">WiFi signal test</a> on a phone held next to the printer to see what it\'s actually receiving.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/05 &mdash; THE HONEST BIT', 'h2': 'When to stop fighting the WiFi', 'html': '<p>Sometimes the smartest fix is a cable. Most mesh nodes have a spare Ethernet port — if your printer has an Ethernet socket and lives near a node, one cheap cable gives you a rock-solid connection that never negotiates bands, never changes address and never roams. A USB cable to one computer is even simpler for a household where only one machine prints. Neither is elegant; both end the fight for good, for the price of a cable.</p><p>Be honest about age, too. If the printer is ten-plus years old, has been temperamental on every router it\'s met, and now needs an afternoon of coaxing onto mesh, the coaxing may cost more in patience than the printer is worth. We\'ll tell you straight either way: don\'t buy a new printer just because one setting needs changing — but don\'t spend a weekend on a machine that was already on its way out.</p><p>And if you\'ve tried the lot and it still won\'t behave, that\'s exactly what we\'re for. We fix most of these remotely, no fix no fee — start at <a href="/printer-support/">printer support</a> or call 01202 775566.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': "Why won't my printer connect to mesh WiFi when my phone connects fine?", 'a': "Usually because your phone can use both WiFi bands and your printer can't. Most mesh systems broadcast one network name covering 2.4GHz and 5GHz, and steer each device to whichever band the system thinks best. Phones cope; many printers only have a 2.4GHz radio, and some fumble the negotiation entirely — they either can't see the network or fail partway through joining. The fix is to get the printer a clean 2.4GHz connection, ideally via a separate IoT or 2.4GHz network if your system offers one, then pin its IP address so it stays found afterwards."}, {'q': 'How do I connect a 2.4GHz-only printer to a mesh network with one network name?', 'a': "Look in your mesh app for a separate 2.4GHz or IoT network and join the printer to that. Brands name it differently — IoT network, smart-home network, 2.4GHz SSID — and some systems don't offer one at all. If yours doesn't, some apps can temporarily pause the 5GHz band while a stubborn device joins; check the settings or the maker's help pages, as these options move around between versions. It also helps if the phone running the printer's setup app is itself on 2.4GHz at the time, because some printers simply copy whatever connection the phone is using."}, {'q': 'Why does my printer keep going offline since I got mesh WiFi?', 'a': "Most often its IP address keeps changing and your computer is printing to the old one. Each time the printer drops off and rejoins the network, it can come back on a different address — but the driver on your computer still points at the previous one, so the printer shows offline even though it's happily connected. Set a DHCP reservation in your mesh app so the printer always gets the same address, then add the printer on each computer by that IP. If it drops out on any router, not just mesh, that's a different fault with its own guide."}, {'q': 'Should I put my printer on the guest network of my mesh system?', 'a': "Usually not. Guest networks commonly switch on client isolation, meaning devices can reach the internet but can't see each other — so your laptop can't find the printer at all. Dedicated IoT networks vary by brand: many let your main devices talk to what's on them, but some isolate as well, so test a print straight after joining rather than assuming. If your printer is currently sitting on a guest network and showing as unreachable, moving it to the main network is the first thing to try — and it fixes this more often than any driver reinstall does."}, {'q': 'Is it easier to just plug my printer into the mesh with a cable?', 'a': "Honestly, yes — if the geography allows it. Most mesh nodes have a spare Ethernet port, and a wired printer never has to negotiate bands, change addresses or roam between nodes, which removes every mesh-specific fault in one go. A USB cable to a single computer is even simpler if only one machine ever prints. It's less tidy than wireless, but we'd rather you spent a few pounds on a cable than five hours on settings. Keep wireless for printers that genuinely have to sit away from every node — and use the fixes above for those."}], 'crossLinksHtml': '<p><strong>Related guides:</strong> <a href="/mesh-wifi-setup-guide/">Mesh WiFi setup guide</a> · <a href="/mesh-wifi-systems-uk/">Mesh WiFi systems UK</a> · <a href="/why-does-my-printer-keep-disconnecting/">Why does my printer keep disconnecting?</a> · <a href="/wifi-troubleshooting/">WiFi troubleshooting</a> · <a href="/wifi-signal-test/">WiFi signal test</a> · <a href="/printer-support/">Printer support</a></p>', 'primaryCta': ['Get it fixed remotely', '/remote-support/'], 'secondaryCta': ['Call 01202 775566', 'tel:+441202775566'], 'schemaKind': 'service'}, {'slug': 'onedrive-keeps-asking-to-sign-in', 'title': 'OneDrive Keeps Asking to Sign In? Fixes | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'OneDrive keeps asking you to sign in on Windows 11? Clear cached credentials, unlink and relink, or reset OneDrive — plus when to call us. Bournemouth experts.', 'ogTitle': 'OneDrive Keeps Asking You to Sign In? How to Break the Loop', 'crumbName': 'OneDrive sign-in loop', 'eyebrow': '// ONEDRIVE · WINDOWS 11', 'h1': 'OneDrive keeps asking me to sign in — how to break the loop', 'lede': "You sign in, OneDrive is happy for an hour — then the sign-in box is back. This credential loop is one of the most common OneDrive complaints on Windows 11, and it's almost always fixable: stale saved passwords, a work and personal account arguing, or a wobbly OneDrive install. Your files in the cloud are safe throughout. Here's the order we'd try things in, and when to stop and call us.", 'chips': ['Trading since 1995', '4.9★ Google rating', 'No fix, no fee'], 'ctaHead': 'Still stuck in the sign-in loop?', 'ctaSub': "We'll connect remotely, watch it happen and fix the real cause — no fix, no fee. Call 01202 775566.", 'serviceName': 'OneDrive Sign-In Loop Fix', 'sections': [{'eyebrow': '/01 &mdash; THE SYMPTOM', 'h2': "Signing in, again and again — what's actually happening", 'html': '<p>You put your password in, OneDrive settles down — and then, an hour or a day later, the "Sign in" box is back. If OneDrive keeps asking you to sign in on Windows 11, you\'re stuck in what\'s usually a <strong>credential loop</strong>, and it nearly always has one of five causes. We\'ll walk through the fixes in the order that sorts the most machines first.</p><ul><li><strong>Stale cached credentials</strong> — Windows keeps handing OneDrive an old saved sign-in that Microsoft\'s servers no longer accept.</li><li><strong>Two accounts fighting</strong> — a work and a personal Microsoft account both trying to hold the same seat.</li><li><strong>Expired security tokens</strong> — especially on work accounts, where the organisation\'s security policy can force regular re-authentication.</li><li><strong>A corrupted OneDrive install.</strong></li><li><strong>A wrong clock</strong> — sign-in tokens are time-sensitive, so a drifting PC clock quietly breaks them.</li></ul><p>One reassurance before you touch anything: everything already synced to OneDrive is safe on Microsoft\'s servers. A sign-in loop pauses new changes syncing; it doesn\'t delete a thing. For other sync misbehaviour beyond sign-in, our wider <a href="/onedrive-problems/">OneDrive problems guide</a> covers the rest.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/02 &mdash; FIX 1', 'h2': 'Sign out properly and clear the cached credentials', 'html': '<p>The single most effective fix is a <em>proper</em> sign-out — not just closing the window, but clearing the saved credentials Windows keeps re-serving.</p><ol><li><strong>Quit OneDrive fully.</strong> Click the cloud icon near the clock, open its settings menu and choose the option to quit or close OneDrive (Microsoft moves this wording around between versions, but it\'s always there).</li><li><strong>Open Credential Manager.</strong> Type "Credential Manager" into the Start menu search, open it, and choose <strong>Windows Credentials</strong>.</li><li><strong>Remove the OneDrive entries.</strong> Look for entries whose names begin "OneDrive Cached Credential" and remove them. If Office apps on the same account are misbehaving too, the "MicrosoftAccount" and Office entries for that address can go as well — Windows simply asks you to sign in fresh next time.</li><li><strong>Restart the PC</strong>, open OneDrive and sign in once with the correct account.</li></ol><p>This forces Windows to fetch a brand-new token instead of recycling the dud one. For a lot of machines, that\'s the whole fix — the nagging stops the same day.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/03 &mdash; FIX 2', 'h2': 'Unlink the account, then relink — or reset OneDrive', 'html': "<p>If the loop survives a credential clear, break and remake the link between this PC and your account.</p><p><strong>Unlink and relink.</strong> In OneDrive's settings, under the Account section, there's an option to <strong>unlink this PC</strong>. It sounds drastic; it isn't. Your files stay in the OneDrive folder on the computer and stay in the cloud — unlinking only severs the sync relationship. Sign back in, point OneDrive at the same folder when it asks, and it re-checks what's there rather than re-downloading everything.</p><p><strong>Reset OneDrive.</strong> Microsoft also publishes a reset command. Press <strong>Windows key + R</strong> and run:</p><p><em>%localappdata%\\Microsoft\\OneDrive\\onedrive.exe /reset</em></p><p>The cloud icon disappears for a minute or two, then OneDrive restarts and rebuilds its settings. If Windows says it can't find the file, don't panic and don't guess — on some machines OneDrive is installed in a different folder, and the reset simply hasn't run. That's a reasonable moment to get a second pair of eyes rather than trying commands from random forums. One more thing worth knowing: if the cloud icon has not reappeared after a couple of minutes, start OneDrive yourself from the Start menu — the reset has still worked, it just did not relaunch on its own. A reset also forgets niceties like which folders you chose to sync, so expect to set those again.</p>"}, {'eyebrow': '/04 &mdash; FIX 3', 'h2': "Two accounts fighting — and a clock that's drifted", 'html': '<p>Two quieter causes catch a lot of people out.</p><p><strong>Read the sign-in box carefully.</strong> If you have both a personal Microsoft account and a work or school account on the same PC, the prompt that keeps appearing may not be for the account you think. Check the email address shown on it — many "loops" are actually a second account, sometimes one you\'d forgotten about, asking for its own password. If an old work account you no longer use is the culprit, removing it in Windows\' account settings (under the accounts used by email and other apps — the exact wording moves between versions) ends the nagging.</p><p><strong>Work accounts can be forced to re-authenticate.</strong> Organisations can set security policies — Conditional Access, sign-in frequency rules — that deliberately expire your session on a schedule. Nothing on your PC overrides that, and it isn\'t a fault. If the interval feels unworkable, it\'s a conversation with whoever runs the Microsoft 365 tenant; that\'s exactly what our <a href="/microsoft-365-support/">Microsoft 365 support</a> handles for local firms.</p><p><strong>Check the clock.</strong> Sign-in tokens are time-sensitive. In the Date &amp; time settings, make sure the option to set time automatically is on. A desktop whose clock drifts after every shutdown often has a failing motherboard battery — a cheap part causing an expensive-looking symptom.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/05 &mdash; HONEST ADVICE', 'h2': "When it's not worth fighting it — and what we'd do", 'html': '<p>If you\'ve cleared the credentials, unlinked and relinked, reset OneDrive and checked for a second account — and the box <em>still</em> comes back — stop. The remaining causes (a damaged Windows user profile, a policy set on the work side, an install that needs removing properly) are fiddly to diagnose blind, and hours of forum-hopping rarely beats twenty minutes of someone watching it happen live.</p><p>Two things we\'d genuinely warn against:</p><ul><li><strong>Don\'t delete the local OneDrive folder in frustration.</strong> OneDrive can mirror deletions to the cloud, so this is the one move that can turn an annoying bug into actual data loss.</li><li><strong>Don\'t keep reinstalling.</strong> The stale credential usually lives in Windows, not in OneDrive itself, so reinstalling the same app over the same problem seldom changes anything.</li></ul><p>This is exactly the sort of job our <a href="/remote-support/">remote support</a> exists for — we connect, watch the loop happen and fix the real cause, on a no-fix-no-fee basis. And if sign-in gremlins are a recurring theme across several machines, our <a href="/monthly-it-support/">monthly IT support</a> keeps them quietly handled before anyone loses a morning to them.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Why does OneDrive keep asking me to sign in on Windows 11?', 'a': "Most often because Windows is caching an old sign-in credential that Microsoft's servers no longer accept, so every fresh sign-in gets overwritten by the stale one. Other regular causes are a work and a personal account clashing on the same PC, an organisation's security policy deliberately expiring work sessions, a corrupted OneDrive installation, or a PC clock that has drifted — sign-in tokens are time-sensitive. Clearing the OneDrive entries in Windows Credential Manager and signing in fresh fixes the largest share of cases, which is why it's the first thing worth trying."}, {'q': 'Will I lose my files if I unlink or reset OneDrive?', 'a': "No — unlinking this PC or running OneDrive's reset only breaks the sync connection; it doesn't delete files from your computer or from the cloud. When you sign back in, OneDrive re-checks what's already there rather than starting from scratch. The one thing that genuinely can cause loss is deleting the local OneDrive folder in frustration, because OneDrive may mirror those deletions to the cloud. Leave the folder alone, fix the sign-in, and everything stays intact."}, {'q': 'How do I stop OneDrive asking for my password every time I turn on my computer?', 'a': 'Clear the stale credentials Windows keeps re-serving: quit OneDrive, open Credential Manager from the Start menu, choose Windows Credentials, remove the entries beginning "OneDrive Cached Credential", restart, and sign in once more. That forces Windows to store a brand-new token instead of recycling the rejected one, and for most machines the daily nagging stops there. If it carries on, unlink and relink the account in OneDrive\'s settings — and if it still persists after that, something deeper is wrong and it\'s worth having someone look remotely.'}, {'q': 'Why does my work OneDrive keep signing me out when my personal one is fine?', 'a': "Because work and school accounts can be governed by the organisation's security policies — things like Conditional Access and sign-in frequency rules — which deliberately expire your session on a schedule and force you to re-authenticate. Your personal account has no such policy, so it stays signed in. This isn't a fault on your PC, and nothing you change locally will override it. If the re-authentication interval is disruptive, the setting lives with whoever administers the Microsoft 365 tenant, and it can usually be tuned to something more sensible."}, {'q': 'Should I just uninstall and reinstall OneDrive to fix the sign-in loop?', 'a': "Not as a first step — the stale credential that causes most sign-in loops is stored by Windows, not inside the OneDrive app, so a reinstall often puts the same problem straight back. Clear the cached credentials in Credential Manager first, then try unlinking and relinking the account, then OneDrive's built-in reset. A full remove-and-reinstall is a reasonable last resort for a genuinely corrupted install, but done in frustration it mostly costs time. If you've reached that point, a quick remote session is usually faster than another reinstall."}, {'q': 'Are my OneDrive files safe while it keeps asking me to sign in?', 'a': "Yes — everything that had already synced before the loop began is safe on Microsoft's servers, and you can confirm that by signing in at the OneDrive website from any browser. What a sign-in loop does do is pause syncing, so new or changed files on the affected PC stop uploading until you're signed in again. That's the real urgency: fix the loop reasonably promptly so recent work isn't sitting only on one machine — but don't panic about the files already in the cloud."}], 'crossLinksHtml': '<p><strong>More help:</strong> <a href="/onedrive-problems/">OneDrive problems &amp; fixes</a> · <a href="/microsoft-365-support/">Microsoft 365 support</a> · <a href="/remote-support/">Remote support (no fix, no fee)</a> · <a href="/monthly-it-support/">Monthly IT support</a></p>', 'primaryCta': ['Get it fixed remotely', '/remote-support/'], 'secondaryCta': ['Call 01202 775566', 'tel:+441202775566'], 'schemaKind': 'service'}, {'slug': 'windows-11-cant-see-network-computers', 'title': "Windows 11 Can't See Other Computers | 365 Techies", 'metaDesc': "Windows 11 can't see other computers on the network? The real causes — Public profile, discovery off, 24H2 SMB changes — and honest fixes from 365 Techies.", 'ogTitle': "Windows 11 Can't See Other Computers on the Network? The Real Fixes", 'crumbName': "Can't See Network PCs", 'eyebrow': '// WINDOWS 11 FIX GUIDE', 'h1': "Windows 11 Can't See Other Computers on the Network", 'lede': "Your PCs all worked together last month — now File Explorer's Network view is empty, or one machine has simply vanished. Windows 11, especially since the 24H2 update, has quietly tightened the rules on file sharing, and it keeps breaking small-office setups that used to just work. Here's what actually causes it, the fixes in the order we'd try them, and an honest word on when peer-to-peer sharing isn't worth saving.", 'chips': ['Trading since 1995', '4.9★ rated on Google', 'No fix, no fee'], 'ctaHead': "Still can't see your other computers?", 'ctaSub': "We diagnose sharing and network problems remotely most days — no fix, no fee. Call 01202 775566 or book a remote session and we'll take a proper look.", 'serviceName': 'Windows 11 Network &amp; File Sharing Support', 'sections': [{'eyebrow': '/01 &mdash; THE SYMPTOM', 'h2': "Your PCs have vanished from the Network view — here's the shape of it", 'html': '<p>You open File Explorer, click <strong>Network</strong>, and the other computers simply aren\'t there. Or one machine can see everyone else but nobody can see it. Or a shared folder that worked for years now throws a "Windows cannot access" error. If that\'s you, you\'re in the right place.</p><p>In small offices and homes this almost always comes down to one of four things, and we\'ll take them in the order we check them on real callouts:</p><ol><li><strong>The network profile</strong> — Windows has decided your own network is "Public" and gone quiet on purpose.</li><li><strong>Network discovery</strong> — the machinery that lets PCs announce themselves is switched off or has stalled.</li><li><strong>Deliberate security tightening</strong> — Windows 11, particularly since the 24H2 update, now blocks older, less secure ways of sharing.</li><li><strong>The machines aren\'t really on the same network</strong> — guest Wi-Fi, mesh kits and second routers quietly split a network in two.</li></ol><p>One honest note before we start: the Network view in File Explorer has always been unreliable, even on perfectly healthy networks. A PC missing from the list doesn\'t necessarily mean you can\'t connect to it — so we\'ll also show you how to reach a machine directly, which often works even when the browsing list is empty.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/02 &mdash; FIRST CHECKS', 'h2': 'Is the network set to Private — and is discovery actually on?', 'html': '<p>Windows treats every network as either <strong>Public</strong> or <strong>Private</strong>, and on a Public network it deliberately hides your PC and refuses incoming file sharing. That\'s sensible on café Wi-Fi and maddening in your own office — and Windows sometimes flips a network back to Public after an update or a router change, without asking.</p><p>Open Settings, go to Network &amp; internet, and click into the properties of the connection you\'re actually using. You\'re looking for the <strong>network profile type</strong> setting: set it to <strong>Private</strong> on a network you trust. Menu wording moves between Windows versions, so if the labels look different, searching Settings for "network profile" will land you in the right place.</p><p>While you\'re there, check <strong>network discovery</strong> and <strong>file and printer sharing</strong> are switched on for private networks — they live under the advanced sharing settings. Do this on <em>every</em> machine involved; it only takes one PC sitting on Public to make a share vanish.</p><p>One more quiet culprit: discovery depends on a handful of background Windows services, and they do occasionally stall. Restarting both machines after changing these settings sounds like a cliché, but it genuinely resets the discovery machinery and is worth doing before you dig any deeper.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/03 &mdash; THE 24H2 CHANGE', 'h2': 'Windows 11 has tightened file sharing on purpose', 'html': '<p>If everything broke after an update, that\'s not your imagination. Windows 11\'s 24H2 release made two deliberate security changes: connections to shared folders now expect <strong>SMB signing</strong>, and <strong>guest access</strong> — shares that let anyone connect without a username and password — is disabled. That silently breaks a lot of older NAS drives and shares set up years ago "without passwords, to keep it simple".</p><p>The right fix is to bring the share up to standard: create a proper user account and password on the NAS or the sharing PC, update the device\'s firmware if it has any available, and connect using those credentials instead of as a guest.</p><p>You <em>can</em> re-enable insecure guest logons through Group Policy or the registry, and plenty of forum posts will cheerfully tell you to. We\'d call that a <strong>last resort</strong>: it switches off a protection that exists for a reason and leaves that share open to anything else on your network. On a home network with one elderly NAS, it\'s a judgement call. In a business, we wouldn\'t do it.</p><p>We\'ve covered the update-specific side in more depth in our guide to <a href="/shared-folders-not-working-after-windows-11-24h2-update/">shared folders not working after the 24H2 update</a>.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/04 &mdash; NAMES & SUBNETS', 'h2': "When the name fails, try the address — and check it's really one network", 'html': '<p>Here\'s the quickest diagnostic we know. Find the sharing PC\'s IP address — it\'s shown in the connection\'s details in Settings, or type <em>ipconfig</em> at a Command Prompt. Then, on the other machine, put <strong>\\\\192.168.1.50</strong> (using the real address) into File Explorer\'s address bar.</p><p>If the address works but the computer\'s <em>name</em> doesn\'t, sharing is actually fine — you have a <strong>name resolution</strong> problem. The old ways Windows found names on a local network have been wound down over the years, and name lookup is now the flakiest part of the whole system. Connecting by IP, or mapping a drive to the IP address, sidesteps it entirely. If mapped drives then start dropping out on you, that\'s a separate known nuisance — see our guide to <a href="/mapped-network-drive-keeps-disconnecting/">mapped network drives that keep disconnecting</a>.</p><p>If the IP address doesn\'t work either, check the machines are genuinely on the same network. Compare their addresses: if the first three groups of numbers don\'t match — say 192.168.1.x on one and 192.168.4.x on the other — they can\'t reach each other. The usual culprits are one PC on the router\'s <strong>guest Wi-Fi</strong>, a mesh kit running in router mode behind another router, a second router added for signal, or an "isolation" setting on the Wi-Fi. Everything needs to sit behind one router doing the addressing.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/05 &mdash; HONEST ADVICE', 'h2': "When it's not worth fighting — and what we'd do instead", 'html': '<p>Here\'s the truth most fix-it guides skip: Microsoft has spent a decade dismantling casual PC-to-PC sharing. HomeGroup was removed, the old SMB1 protocol was retired, and now guest access and unsigned connections are blocked too. Each change was made for sound security reasons — and each one broke setups that used to just work. Peer-to-peer sharing between Windows machines is fragile <em>by design</em> now, and every feature update is another chance for it to fall over.</p><p>For a home user moving the odd file about, honestly: a shared cloud folder — or even a USB stick — may cost you far less grief than keeping Windows shares alive.</p><p>For a business, we\'ll say it plainly: if the company\'s files live on one person\'s PC and everyone else maps to it, you\'re one Windows update, one failed drive or one annual leave away from a very bad week. A small <strong>NAS</strong> with proper user accounts, or Microsoft 365\'s built-in file sharing, gives you permissions, backups, and files that don\'t disappear when a PC is off. Setting that up — and keeping it healthy — is exactly the sort of thing covered by our <a href="/business-it-support-subscriptions/">business IT support subscriptions</a>.</p><p>And if you\'d rather not lose an afternoon to it, we can usually diagnose sharing problems in a single <a href="/remote-support/">remote support</a> session — no fix, no fee.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': "Why can't Windows 11 see other computers on my network?", 'a': "The most common cause is that Windows has set your network profile to Public, which deliberately hides your PC and blocks file sharing. Check every machine is set to Private and that network discovery and file and printer sharing are switched on. If it broke after an update, Windows 11's 24H2 release also disabled guest access to shares and expects signed connections, which silently breaks older NAS drives and password-free shares. And bear in mind the Network view in File Explorer has always been unreliable — try connecting to the other PC directly by name or IP address before assuming sharing itself is broken."}, {'q': 'How do I change my network from Public to Private in Windows 11?', 'a': 'Open Settings, go to Network &amp; internet, click into the properties of your current Wi-Fi or Ethernet connection, and set the network profile type to Private. Microsoft moves these menus around between versions, so if the labels look different, search Settings for "network profile" and you\'ll land in the right place. Only set a network to Private if you genuinely trust it — your own home or office, never a café or hotel. Afterwards, check network discovery and file and printer sharing are on under the advanced sharing settings, and repeat all of this on every machine involved.'}, {'q': 'Did the Windows 11 24H2 update break my network file sharing?', 'a': "Quite possibly, yes — and it was deliberate rather than a bug. 24H2 expects SMB signing and disables insecure guest logons by default, which breaks connections to older NAS boxes and to shares set up without a username and password. The proper fix is to update the device's firmware and connect with a real account and password rather than as a guest. Rolling the security settings back is possible but weakens your protection, so we treat it as a last resort. We've written a separate guide on shared folders not working after 24H2 with the full detail."}, {'q': 'Is it safe to turn insecure guest logons back on in Windows 11?', 'a': "It usually works, but we'd treat it as a last resort. Guest logons let anything on your network open the share without a password, and Microsoft disabled them partly because Windows has no way to verify a guest connection hasn't been tampered with. On a home network with one elderly NAS that can't do better, it's a calculated risk some people accept. In a business handling customer or financial data, we wouldn't do it: create a proper account on the device instead, or replace the device if it genuinely can't manage one."}, {'q': 'Do all my computers need to be on the same network to share files?', 'a': "Yes — for normal Windows file sharing, every machine needs to be on the same subnet, which in practice means connected to the same router or mesh system. Compare the IP addresses: if the first three groups of numbers don't match, the machines can't reach each other. Setups get split without anyone noticing: one PC on the router's guest Wi-Fi, a mesh kit running in router mode behind another router, a second router added as an extender, or an isolation setting on the Wi-Fi. Getting everything back onto one network — one box doing the addressing — fixes it."}, {'q': 'Should my business use a NAS instead of sharing folders between PCs?', 'a': "In most cases, yes. Peer-to-peer sharing between Windows PCs is fragile by design now — Microsoft has removed HomeGroup, retired SMB1 and blocked guest access, and every feature update risks breaking what's left. A small NAS with proper user accounts, or Microsoft 365's built-in file sharing, gives you permissions, backups, and files that don't vanish when one PC is switched off or mid-update. The cost is modest set against a morning of lost work each time sharing falls over. We set these up regularly for small Dorset businesses — and we'll tell you honestly if your current setup is actually fine."}], 'crossLinksHtml': '<p><strong>Related guides:</strong> <a href="/shared-folders-not-working-after-windows-11-24h2-update/">Shared folders not working after the 24H2 update</a> · <a href="/mapped-network-drive-keeps-disconnecting/">Mapped network drive keeps disconnecting</a> · <a href="/business-it-support-subscriptions/">Business IT support subscriptions</a> · <a href="/remote-support/">Remote support</a></p>', 'primaryCta': ['Get it fixed remotely', '/remote-support/'], 'secondaryCta': ['Call 01202 775566', 'tel:+441202775566'], 'schemaKind': 'service'}, {'slug': 'office-wifi-keeps-dropping-out', 'title': 'Office WiFi Keeps Dropping Out for Everyone? | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'Whole-office WiFi drops mean infrastructure, not devices. The six real causes — and the free room-by-room survey from 365 Techies that shows which is yours.', 'ogTitle': "Office WiFi Keeps Dropping Out for Everyone? Here's the Real Cause", 'crumbName': 'Office WiFi Dropping Out', 'eyebrow': '// BUSINESS WIFI', 'h1': 'Office WiFi Keeps Dropping Out for Everyone?', 'lede': "When one laptop plays up, it's the laptop. When the whole office loses WiFi at once — every laptop, every phone, the card machine — the fault is in the infrastructure: the router, a switch, the airwaves, or the broadband line behind them. This page works through the usual suspects in the order we'd check them, and explains the free room-by-room survey that shows, in writing, which one is yours.", 'chips': ['Free room-by-room WiFi survey', 'Trading in Dorset since 1995', 'Rated 4.9 on Google'], 'ctaHead': 'Book your free room-by-room WiFi survey', 'ctaSub': "Run our free room-by-room survey while the team is working — it measures what's actually happening and builds a plain-English report. Send it to us and a real person will name the likely cause, free. Call 01202 775566 — no obligation, and we'll tell you if the fix is cheap.", 'serviceName': 'Office WiFi Diagnosis & Survey', 'sections': [{'eyebrow': '/01 &mdash; THE SYMPTOM', 'h2': 'Everyone drops at once? Stop blaming the laptops', 'html': '<p>The WiFi keeps dropping out for everyone — every laptop, every phone, the printer, sometimes the card machine — and then it comes back, until it doesn\'t. If that\'s your office, you can stop troubleshooting individual devices right now: thirty machines don\'t all develop the same fault in the same second. Whole-office drops mean something <em>shared</em> has failed, however briefly — the router, a switch, the airwaves your WiFi travels over, or the broadband line behind the lot.</p><p>That\'s genuinely useful news, because it shrinks the suspect list from thirty devices to about five causes, and each one leaves different fingerprints. Below we walk through them in the order we\'d check them ourselves: an overworked consumer router, an exhausted address pool, channel congestion from neighbouring businesses, a failing PoE switch, and — more often than anyone expects — a broadband line that\'s dropping while the WiFi itself is perfectly healthy.</p><p>If your problem is one stubborn laptop rather than the whole room, start with our general <a href="/wifi-troubleshooting/">WiFi troubleshooting guide</a> instead. If it really is everyone, read on — and know the definitive answer isn\'t guesswork: it\'s a survey done in your building while your team is actually working.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/02 &mdash; SUSPECT ONE', 'h2': "One consumer router doing a whole business's job", 'html': '<p>Most small offices are still running on the router the broadband provider posted out — a box designed for a family. It\'s doing the routing, the firewall, the WiFi and the job of handing every device an address (DHCP), and it was specced for a household\'s worth of gadgets. Ask it to serve twenty staff plus their phones, the printers, the CCTV and every visitor\'s handset, and it runs hot, drops wireless clients under load, and occasionally reboots itself — at which point everyone drops at once.</p><p>The address pool is the quieter version of the same problem. Every device on your network borrows an address from a fixed pool, and on many routers that pool is smaller than people assume — defaults and settings vary by model, which is exactly why we check rather than guess. When the pool fills up, new devices can\'t join at all, and as leases expire and churn, devices that were happily connected start behaving strangely.</p><p>The fingerprint: drops that get worse mid-morning as the office fills, devices showing "connected, no internet", and everything recovering after a router reboot — then sliding back within days. A reboot isn\'t a fix; it\'s a symptom with a schedule.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/03 &mdash; SUSPECTS TWO AND THREE', 'h2': "Crowded channels — and Dorset's radar quirk", 'html': '<p>WiFi is a shared radio, and in a business park or above a high street you\'re sharing it with every firm around you. The older 2.4 GHz band has, in practice, only three channels that don\'t overlap — so when your neighbours\' networks pile onto the same ones, everybody\'s WiFi degrades together, and it\'s worst at exactly the hours you need it most.</p><p>The faster 5 GHz band has more room, but one quirk matters enormously around here: many of its channels are shared with radar. The rules (called DFS) require an access point that detects radar — or anything that resembles it — to leave that channel immediately. When that happens, every device on the access point drops while it finds a new home. Brief, random, whole-office cut-outs that heal themselves within a minute or two are the classic signature, and being near the Dorset coast and Bournemouth Airport puts radar events firmly on the local suspect list.</p><p>You can\'t see any of this from a laptop\'s WiFi menu. It shows up when someone measures the airwaves room by room — which is exactly what our free <a href="/wifi-signal-test/">WiFi signal test</a> does, at your desks, during working hours.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/04 &mdash; SUSPECTS FOUR AND FIVE', 'h2': 'The failing switch, and the line drop everyone blames on WiFi', 'html': '<p>Two causes get missed constantly. The first is the PoE switch — the box that feeds power and data to ceiling-mounted access points through the network cable. When one starts failing, or overheats in a warm cupboard, it can restart the access points it powers, and all the WiFi in the building vanishes together for a minute or two. If your drops cluster on warm afternoons or busy periods, the switch deserves a hard look.</p><p>The second is the most common misdiagnosis we see: the broadband line dropping while the WiFi stays up. Your bars stay full — you\'re still connected to the router — but the internet behind it has gone, so it <em>feels</em> like a WiFi fault. Here\'s the order that settles it:</p><ol><li>When a drop hits, check a wired computer. If it loses internet at the same moment, the wireless is innocent.</li><li>Read the router\'s log — most routers keep one, usually under a name like "system log" (menus move between models). Repeated WAN drops or reconnection entries mean the line itself is failing.</li><li>Only if wired connections stay up and the logs are clean is it genuinely a wireless problem.</li></ol><p>If the line turns out to be the culprit, the fix is a conversation with your provider — and, for offices that can\'t afford the downtime, a <a href="/business-continuity-internet/">backup internet connection</a> that takes over automatically.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/05 &mdash; THE HONEST BIT', 'h2': "When it's not worth fighting it — and what we'd do", 'html': '<p>Here\'s the honest bit: the wrong response to whole-office drops is buying kit on a hunch. We regularly meet offices on their third WiFi booster or second <a href="/mesh-wifi-systems-uk/">consumer mesh kit</a> — decent products in the right home, but in a congested office their wireless links just add more traffic to airwaves that may already be the problem, and none of them do anything for a full address pool, a failing switch or a dropping line. That\'s not fixing; that\'s paying to guess.</p><p>Sometimes the real fix costs almost nothing — a bigger address pool, a better channel, one setting on the router. When that\'s the case, we\'ll tell you, and you\'ll spend a fraction of what another gadget would have cost. Sometimes the building genuinely needs proper access points. The point is to know which <em>before</em> money leaves your account.</p><p>That\'s why the first step is a free room-by-room survey with your team in the building — run it yourself, in a browser, with our <a href="/wifi-signal-test/">free WiFi survey tool</a>, then send us the results for a free human review. Congestion, load and line drops only show themselves under real use, so we measure during a normal working day. You get a written, plain-English report naming the cause and what fixing it involves — and if the honest answer is "your kit is fine, it\'s the line", the report says exactly that.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/06 &mdash; THE PROPER FIX', 'h2': 'What a real access-point install looks like', 'html': '<p>When the survey does point at the wireless itself, here\'s what a proper fix looks like — and it isn\'t a bigger aerial. A <a href="/business-wifi-installation/">business WiFi installation</a> means cabled access points, usually ceiling-mounted, each fed power and data through one network cable from a healthy PoE switch. Placement comes from the survey, not guesswork: the access points go where your people, walls and dead spots actually are.</p><p>They all broadcast one network name, so laptops and phones move around the building without dropping. A router built for business handles a properly sized address pool, visitors go on a separate guest network that can\'t see your systems, and channels are planned around what your neighbours are doing rather than left on automatic.</p><p>For a typical small office it\'s usually a modest, planned job — though cabling and building quirks vary, which is why we quote from the survey rather than a price list. The result is the thing good office WiFi should be: boring. Nobody talks about it, nobody reboots anything, and "is the WiFi down?" disappears from the group chat. If this page sounds like your office, call 01202 775566 — the survey is free, and so is being told you don\'t need us yet.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Why does the WiFi keep dropping out for everyone in my office at the same time?', 'a': "Because something shared is failing — not the individual computers. When every device drops together, the cause sits in the infrastructure: an overloaded consumer router, a full DHCP address pool, channel congestion with neighbouring businesses, a failing PoE switch, a radar (DFS) event forcing your 5 GHz channel to move, or the broadband line dropping behind WiFi that's actually fine. The quickest clue is a wired computer: if it loses internet at the same moments the wireless does, the problem is the line or the router, not the WiFi at all."}, {'q': "How do I tell if it's the WiFi or the broadband that keeps dropping?", 'a': "Check a wired device the moment a drop happens. If a computer plugged in by cable loses internet at the same time as the wireless ones, the WiFi is innocent — the broadband line or the router itself is dropping. If wired connections stay up while wireless falls over, it genuinely is a WiFi problem. The router's own log usually settles it: repeated WAN or reconnection entries mean the line is dropping, and that's a conversation with your provider — or a case for a backup connection — rather than new access points."}, {'q': 'How many devices can one router handle in an office?', 'a': "Fewer than most offices are asking of it — and the honest answer is that it varies by model. The consumer router your provider supplied was designed around a household's worth of devices, while an office of twenty staff can easily carry sixty or more once you count phones, printers, CCTV and visitors. It isn't only a hard limit, either: as device counts climb, a consumer router's WiFi gets slower and less stable well before it refuses connections, and its address pool can simply run out. If drops track with how full the office is, the router is the prime suspect."}, {'q': 'Why does our office WiFi cut out at random for a minute and then come back?', 'a': 'Brief, random, whole-office drops that fix themselves are a classic sign of a DFS radar event on the 5 GHz band. Many 5 GHz WiFi channels are shared with radar, and the rules require an access point that detects radar — or something resembling it — to leave the channel immediately, cutting everyone off while it moves. Near the Dorset coast and Bournemouth Airport this is a genuinely plausible trigger. The other candidate is a router or switch quietly rebooting; router logs and a room-by-room survey tell the two apart.'}, {'q': 'Will a mesh WiFi system fix drop-outs in an office?', 'a': "Sometimes, but often not — and it's worth knowing which before you spend. Consumer mesh kits are excellent in homes, but in a congested office the wireless links between mesh units add more traffic to airwaves that may already be the problem, and they do nothing for a full address pool, a failing switch or a dropping broadband line. Offices usually do better with cabled access points placed from a survey. Our free room-by-room survey shows whether mesh would genuinely help in your building before you buy anything."}, {'q': 'How much does it cost to fix office WiFi that keeps dropping?', 'a': "Sometimes nothing beyond a settings change — which is why we survey before we quote. A drop caused by a full address pool or a poor channel choice can be fixed in minutes; a failing switch is a modest hardware swap; a full multi-access-point install is a bigger job we'd only recommend if the survey shows you need it. We don't publish a fixed price because buildings differ too much for that to be honest. The room-by-room survey is free, and the written report tells you which end of the scale you're on before you commit to anything."}], 'crossLinksHtml': '<p><strong>Related guides:</strong> <a href="/business-wifi-installation/">Business WiFi installation</a> · <a href="/wifi-signal-test/">Free WiFi signal test</a> · <a href="/wifi-troubleshooting/">WiFi troubleshooting</a> · <a href="/business-continuity-internet/">Backup internet for business</a> · <a href="/mesh-wifi-systems-uk/">Mesh WiFi systems guide</a></p>', 'primaryCta': ['Talk to us about your office', '/contact/'], 'secondaryCta': ['Call 01202 775566', 'tel:+441202775566'], 'schemaKind': 'service'}, {'slug': 'printer-says-wifi-password-incorrect', 'title': 'Printer Says WiFi Password Incorrect? Fixes | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'Printer rejecting a WiFi password you know is right? The real causes — 2.4GHz bands, WPA3, channel 13, old firmware — and the fixes in order, from 365 Techies.', 'ogTitle': "Printer says the WiFi password is incorrect — but it's right", 'crumbName': 'Printer says password incorrect', 'eyebrow': '// PRINTER TROUBLESHOOTING', 'h1': "Printer says the WiFi password is incorrect — but it's right", 'lede': "You've checked the router sticker, typed the password with painful care, and the printer still says it's wrong. In our experience the password usually is right — the printer is failing for another reason and blaming the password, because that's the only error message it has. Here's what's actually going on, from 2.4GHz-only radios to WPA3 and the UK's channel 13, and the fixes in the order we'd try them.", 'chips': ['No fix, no fee', 'Rated 4.9 on Google', 'Family-run since 1995'], 'ctaHead': 'Still being told your password is wrong?', 'ctaSub': "We'll connect remotely, see what your network and printer are actually doing, and sort it — no fix, no fee. Call 01202 775566.", 'serviceName': 'Printer WiFi troubleshooting', 'sections': [{'eyebrow': '/01 &mdash; START HERE', 'h2': 'Yes — the password can be right and still get rejected', 'html': '<p>You\'ve typed the WiFi password into the printer slowly, character by character, checked it against the sticker on the router — and the printer still insists it\'s incorrect. You know it isn\'t, and you\'re probably not wrong. On most printers, <strong>"incorrect password" is a catch-all error</strong>: the printer often can\'t tell the difference between a wrong password and a network it simply couldn\'t join, so it blames the password because that\'s the only message it has.</p><p>The real culprit is usually one of a handful of things: a 2.4GHz-only printer meeting a modern combined-band network, a security mode the printer doesn\'t speak, a UK WiFi channel its firmware can\'t even see, or stale settings left over from a previous router. Before anything else, it <em>is</em> worth one more careful retype — printer keypads make slips easy and WiFi passwords are case-sensitive — but once you\'ve done that honestly, stop retyping and work through the causes below in order. One of them is almost certainly your problem.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/02 &mdash; THE BAND TRAP', 'h2': 'Most printers only speak 2.4GHz — and modern routers hide it', 'html': '<p>Most home printers — especially budget and older models — only support 2.4GHz WiFi. Modern routers and mesh systems usually broadcast 2.4GHz and 5GHz under a single network name and quietly steer devices between the bands. That\'s great for phones and laptops, but a 2.4GHz-only printer can get caught in the middle of that negotiation, fail to join, and report the failure as a password error.</p><p>Two things help. First, check whether your router or mesh app lets you split the bands into two separately named networks, or offers a pairing mode that temporarily favours 2.4GHz for new devices — many do, though the setting\'s name and location vary by brand. Join the printer to the 2.4GHz name and it will usually stay put. If you\'re on a mesh system, our <a href="/mesh-wifi-setup-guide/">mesh WiFi setup guide</a> covers band settings in more detail.</p><p>Second, position matters: 2.4GHz travels further than 5GHz, but a printer at the very edge of coverage can fail to authenticate at all. And if the printer connects fine but keeps dropping off afterwards, that\'s a different fault — see <a href="/why-does-my-printer-keep-disconnecting/">why your printer keeps disconnecting</a>.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/03 &mdash; SECURITY & CHANNELS', 'h2': "WPA3-only networks, hidden names and the UK's channel 13", 'html': '<p>WPA3 is the newest WiFi security standard, and some routers now ship set to WPA3-only. Plenty of printers — including some only a few years old — only speak WPA2, and a WPA2 device knocking on a WPA3-only door gets rejected in a way many printers report as a bad password. The fix is transitional (mixed) mode, usually labelled something like <strong>WPA2/WPA3</strong>, which lets old and new devices coexist. Interfaces move around between routers and firmware versions, so look for the wireless <em>security mode</em> setting rather than a specific menu path.</p><p>Two more invisible walls. If you\'ve hidden your network name, many printers handle that badly: you\'d need to type the SSID manually, exactly, case-sensitive — or better, unhide it while you connect (hiding adds very little real security anyway). And here\'s a UK-specific one: routers here can legally use 2.4GHz channels 12 and 13, but printers with US-region firmware stop listening at channel 11. If your router has auto-picked channel 13, the printer can\'t see your network <em>at all</em>. Manually setting the 2.4GHz channel to 11 or below cures it. Our <a href="/wifi-troubleshooting/">WiFi troubleshooting guide</a> covers channels in more depth.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/04 &mdash; FIRMWARE QUIRKS', 'h2': "Passwords the printer can't digest — and memories it won't let go", 'html': "<p>Older printer firmware can be fussy about the password itself. Some models mishandle certain symbols — quotation marks, spaces, the £ sign — and some accept fewer characters than your router allows, silently truncating a long passphrase and then, of course, failing. If your password is long and symbol-heavy, that alone can be the whole problem.</p><p>The other quiet saboteur is memory. If you've replaced your router but kept the same network name — common when a provider sends a like-for-like swap — the printer may cling to saved settings from the old router: old password, old security type, sometimes an old band preference. It looks like it's trying to join your network; it's actually trying to rejoin a network that no longer exists. The cure is to clear the printer's stored network settings — the option is usually called something like <strong>restore network defaults</strong>, though names vary by maker — so it starts genuinely fresh, then set the connection up from scratch as if the printer were brand new.</p>"}, {'eyebrow': '/05 &mdash; THE FIX ORDER', 'h2': 'Work through it in this order (and where WPS fits)', 'html': "<p>Here's the order we'd work in — cheapest effort first:</p><ol><li>Restart both the printer and the router, then retype the password once, slowly, watching upper and lower case.</li><li>Get the printer onto 2.4GHz — split the bands or use your router's pairing mode.</li><li>Set wireless security to WPA2/WPA3 mixed mode rather than WPA3-only.</li><li>Fix the 2.4GHz channel to 11 or below.</li><li>Unhide the network name, at least while you connect.</li><li>Clear the printer's stored network settings and set it up from scratch.</li></ol><p>If all of that fails and both your router and printer have a <strong>WPS button</strong>, push-button pairing is a legitimate last resort: press the router's button, then the printer's within a couple of minutes, and no password is typed at all. The honest caveat: WPS — particularly its PIN variant — has well-documented security weaknesses, so it's sensible to switch WPS off in your router's settings once the printer is connected, where your router allows it. We wouldn't leave it permanently enabled on a network we look after.</p>"}, {'eyebrow': '/06 &mdash; HONEST ADVICE', 'h2': "When it's not worth fighting — and what we'd do", 'html': '<p>Some fights aren\'t worth having, and we\'ll always tell you which. If your printer is old enough that it only supports WEP or the original WPA, <strong>don\'t downgrade your whole network\'s security to accommodate it</strong> — that protects one elderly printer by exposing every other device you own. Connect it by USB cable instead (or Ethernet, on models that have a port) and share it from a PC, or accept that its wireless days are over.</p><p>Similarly, if you\'ve spent two evenings wrestling a printer that cost very little new, replacement is sometimes the genuinely cheaper answer — and we\'d rather say that plainly than bill you for heroics.</p><p>If you\'d like a hand either way, our <a href="/printer-support/">printer support</a> is remote-first: we connect to your computer, see exactly what your network and printer are doing, and if we can\'t fix it, you don\'t pay. Call <strong>01202 775566</strong> — no-fix-no-fee, and free local collection if any hardware ever needs to come to us.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': "Why does my printer say the WiFi password is incorrect when I know it's right?", 'a': 'Because "incorrect password" is a catch-all error on most printers — it often means the printer couldn\'t join the network for any reason, not that the password was actually wrong. The usual real causes are a 2.4GHz-only printer meeting a network that combines 2.4GHz and 5GHz under one name, a router set to WPA3-only security that an older printer can\'t speak, a UK router using channel 12 or 13 that US-firmware printers can\'t see, or stale settings saved from a previous router. Work through those in order and the "password" error usually disappears.'}, {'q': 'How do I connect a printer that only supports 2.4GHz WiFi?', 'a': "Give it a clear route onto the 2.4GHz band. Many routers and mesh apps let you split the two bands into separately named networks, or offer a pairing mode that temporarily favours 2.4GHz for new devices — the setting's name varies by brand, so look for anything mentioning bands, smart connect or band steering. Join the printer to the 2.4GHz name and it will normally stay put. Once connected, you can usually re-merge the bands without the printer dropping off, though behaviour varies between systems, so check the printer again afterwards."}, {'q': 'Is it safe to use WPS to connect my printer?', 'a': "Push-button WPS is an acceptable last resort, but don't leave it switched on for ever. Pressing the WPS button on the router and then on the printer pairs them without typing a password at all, which neatly sidesteps keypad slips and character problems. The catch is that WPS — especially its PIN variant — has well-documented security weaknesses. Our advice: use the button method to get connected, then disable WPS in your router's settings afterwards if your router allows it. If your router only offers PIN-based WPS, we'd skip it and fix the underlying cause instead."}, {'q': "Why won't my printer connect to my new router?", 'a': 'Usually because the printer is clinging to saved settings from your old router — especially if the new one uses the same network name. The printer tries the old password or old security type, fails, and blames the password. Clear the printer\'s stored network settings — the option is usually called something like "restore network defaults" — and set the connection up from scratch. If it still refuses, check the new router isn\'t set to WPA3-only security, and that its 2.4GHz band is available and using channel 11 or below.'}, {'q': 'Can special characters in my WiFi password stop a printer connecting?', 'a': 'Yes, on some older printers. Ageing firmware can mishandle symbols such as quotation marks, spaces or the £ sign, and some printers accept fewer password characters than your router allows, silently cutting the end off a long passphrase. If your password is long and symbol-heavy, that alone can cause a permanent "incorrect password" loop. Changing your WiFi password to something simpler would fix it, but every other device in the house would then need reconnecting — so treat that as a last resort, or use push-button WPS instead where it\'s available.'}], 'crossLinksHtml': '<p><strong>Related guides:</strong> <a href="/why-does-my-printer-keep-disconnecting/">Why does my printer keep disconnecting?</a> · <a href="/wifi-troubleshooting/">WiFi troubleshooting</a> · <a href="/mesh-wifi-setup-guide/">Mesh WiFi setup guide</a> · <a href="/printer-support/">Printer support</a></p>', 'primaryCta': ['Get it fixed remotely', '/remote-support/'], 'secondaryCta': ['Call 01202 775566', 'tel:+441202775566'], 'schemaKind': 'service'}, {'slug': 'printer-stopped-working-after-changing-wifi-password', 'title': 'Printer Stopped After WiFi Password Change | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'Every device re-joined except the printer? Where to re-enter your new WiFi password on common printers, why scanning breaks separately, and the fix order.', 'ogTitle': "Printer Stopped Working After Changing the WiFi Password? Here's the Fix Order", 'crumbName': 'Printer &amp; WiFi password', 'eyebrow': '// PRINTER FIX GUIDE', 'h1': 'Printer Stopped Working After Changing Your WiFi Password?', 'lede': "You changed your WiFi password — sensibly, perhaps after a scare — and every phone, laptop and tablet asked for the new one and got on with life. The printer didn't ask. It just went quiet. That's normal: printers never prompt for a new password, they simply keep trying the old one. Here's where to re-enter it on the main printer types, why scanning breaks separately, and the right re-securing order if a scam prompted all this.", 'chips': ['No fix, no fee remote help', 'Family-run since 1995', 'Rated 4.9 on Google'], 'ctaHead': "Printer still won't take the new password?", 'ctaSub': 'Call 01202 775566 — with your say-so we connect remotely, see what the printer and router are each doing, and get you printing again. No fix, no fee.', 'serviceName': 'Printer WiFi reconnection support', 'sections': [{'eyebrow': "/01 &mdash; WHAT'S GOING ON", 'h2': "Every device re-joined except the printer — here's why", 'html': '<p>You changed the WiFi password, every phone and laptop asked for the new one and carried on, but the printer just sat there — jobs stuck in the queue, and the computer insisting the printer is offline. Nothing is broken. Printers store the WiFi password once, during setup, and almost none of them ever pop up a message asking for the new one; they quietly keep trying the old password forever.</p><p>Because your router and network name haven\'t changed, this is purely a credential re-entry job: tell the printer the new password once and it should behave. Below we cover the three ways to do that — the printer\'s own setup wizard if it has a screen, the WPS button method if it doesn\'t, and the USB-cable-plus-app route when neither works — plus the separate gotcha that catches people out: printing comes back but scan-to-computer stays broken.</p><p>One check before you start: this page assumes <strong>only the password changed</strong>. If you\'ve switched broadband provider or been sent a new router, the network name has usually changed too, which is a slightly different problem — see our guide to a <a href="/printer-wont-connect-after-changing-broadband/">printer that won\'t connect after changing broadband</a> instead.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/02 &mdash; THE EASY WAY', 'h2': 'Printers with a screen: re-run the wireless setup wizard', 'html': '<p>If your printer has a touchscreen, or a small display with arrow buttons, it can take the new password directly. Look in its settings for something called <strong>Wireless Setup Wizard</strong>, <strong>WiFi Setup</strong> or <strong>Network Setup</strong> — the exact name and menu position move between makes, models and even firmware versions, so hunt for the word "wireless" rather than following a rigid click-path. The wizard scans for nearby networks; choose yours — it will be the same name as before, since only the password changed — and type the new password.</p><p>Two things trip people up here. First, WiFi passwords are case-sensitive and printer keyboards are fiddly: a lowercase l, a capital I and the digit 1 look near-identical on a small screen, as do 0 and O. If it fails once, don\'t assume the method is wrong — re-type it slowly. Second, once the printer re-joins, the jobs that failed earlier are often still jammed in the queue. Open the print queue on your computer, cancel anything sitting there, and send a fresh test page. If the printer joins the network but your computer still shows it offline, see the printing-versus-scanning section below — that\'s usually an address problem, not a password one.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/03 &mdash; NO SCREEN?', 'h2': 'Button-only printers: WPS, or a temporary USB cable', 'html': "<p>Plenty of budget printers have no screen at all — just a WiFi light and a couple of buttons. You have two routes.</p><p><strong>WPS</strong> is the quickest, if your router supports it: press the WPS button on the router, then within a couple of minutes press the WiFi (or WPS) button on the printer, and the two exchange the new password automatically — no typing. The honest caveats: some broadband routers ship with WPS switched off, some have dropped it entirely for security reasons, and the exact button-press — tap versus hold, which light should flash — varies by printer, so a glance at the quick-start leaflet or the maker's website helps.</p><p><strong>The USB route</strong> is the reliable fallback. Connect the printer to your computer with an ordinary USB printer cable, then run the manufacturer's own setup app — HP, Epson, Canon and Brother all offer one as a free download from their official support sites — and use its wireless setup option to send the new password to the printer over the cable. Once it's on the WiFi, unplug the cable.</p><p>If the printer seems stuck in a sulk whatever you try, most models let you reset just their <em>network</em> settings to factory and join fresh — often the cleanest cure for a wedged connection.</p>"}, {'eyebrow': '/04 &mdash; THE GOTCHA', 'h2': 'Printing works again — so why is scanning still broken?', 'html': '<p>This is the bit that catches almost everyone. Printing and scanning travel different roads. When you print, your computer goes looking for the printer. When you scan — especially scan-to-computer from the printer\'s own buttons — the software relies on a saved registration, and that registration often records the printer\'s network address. Re-joining the WiFi can hand the printer a <strong>new</strong> address, so printing recovers while the scan side quietly points at the old one.</p><p>The fix is re-registration, not repair. Re-run the scan setup in the manufacturer\'s app — each maker has its own scan utility, and its name moves between versions, so look for "scan" in their software rather than a specific title. Alternatively, remove the printer in your computer\'s settings and add it again, which refreshes both printing and scanning in one go. If you normally scan from the computer\'s side rather than the printer\'s buttons, that same remove-and-re-add usually sorts it too.</p><p>Nothing is faulty and no new hardware is needed — this is bookkeeping, not breakage. It\'s also why a printer can look "half fixed" for weeks: the half you use daily recovered, and the half you use monthly didn\'t.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/05 &mdash; IF A SCAM STARTED THIS', 'h2': "Changed the password after a scam scare? Good — here's the full order", 'html': '<p>A lot of people change their WiFi password straight after a scam phone call or an alarming pop-up, and it\'s a sensible instinct. But be honest with yourself about what it protects: it stops strangers joining your network, and that\'s all. It does <strong>not</strong> remove any remote-access software a caller talked you into installing, it doesn\'t change your email or banking passwords, and it doesn\'t undo anything that already happened on the computer itself.</p><p>If a scare kicked this off, the WiFi password is step three, not step one. Our order:</p><ol><li>Uninstall any remote-access program the caller had you download, and restart the computer.</li><li>Change your important account passwords, starting with email — it\'s the master key to everything else — ideally from a device the caller never touched.</li><li>Then the router and WiFi passwords (which is what stranded your printer).</li><li>Run a full malware scan, and keep an eye on bank and card statements for a couple of weeks.</li></ol><p>We\'ve written a step-by-step guide for exactly this situation — <a href="/gave-a-scammer-remote-access/">what to do if you gave a scammer remote access</a> — and if you\'d rather a human checked the machine over properly, that\'s a routine job for us, not an embarrassing one. And one thing before anything else: if you paid the caller, read out card details, or online banking was open while they were connected, call your bank first — dial 159, the anti-fraud number that safely connects you to your own bank — before you worry about the printer.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/06 &mdash; STRAIGHT TALK', 'h2': "When it's not worth fighting — and what we'd do", 'html': '<p>Two honest scenarios. First: you\'ve typed the password carefully, run the wizard twice, tried WPS and the USB app, and the printer still won\'t hold a connection. Don\'t burn an evening on it — and please don\'t buy a new printer yet. In our experience this is usually a settings mismatch, not a hardware failure: some newer routers default to security modes that older printers don\'t understand, and that\'s a short job to diagnose, not a reason to bin the machine. With your permission we can connect via <a href="/remote-support/">remote support</a>, see what the printer and router are each doing, and fix the mismatch — no fix, no fee.</p><p>Second, the other side of the honesty coin: if the printer is genuinely ancient, drops off the network every few weeks anyway, and a set of ink costs more than the machine did, we\'ll tell you plainly that reconnecting it isn\'t the best use of your money — and what a sensible replacement costs, before you decide anything. We don\'t sell printers, so we\'ve no reason to push you either way.</p><p>Our <a href="/printer-support/">printer support</a> page covers everything we handle, or call 01202 775566 and talk it through first — that call costs nothing.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': "Why won't my printer connect after I changed my WiFi password?", 'a': "Because the printer is still trying the old password. Printers save the WiFi password once, during setup, and unlike phones and laptops most never prompt you when it changes — they just fail silently and show as offline. The router and network name are unchanged, so nothing is broken: you simply need to re-enter the new password on the printer itself, using its wireless setup wizard, the WPS button method, or a temporary USB connection to the manufacturer's app. Once it re-joins, stuck print jobs usually need cancelling and re-sending."}, {'q': 'How do I put the new WiFi password into my printer?', 'a': "On a printer with a screen, find the setting called something like Wireless Setup Wizard or WiFi Setup — menu names move between makes and models — choose your network and type the new password exactly, because it's case-sensitive and printer keyboards make 1, l and I easy to confuse. On a printer without a screen, either use WPS (press the router's WPS button, then the printer's wireless button) or connect a USB cable temporarily and run the manufacturer's free setup app, which sends the new password across for you."}, {'q': 'Why can I print but not scan to my computer after changing my WiFi password?', 'a': "Because scanning is registered separately, and it often remembers the printer by its old network address. When the printer re-joined your WiFi it may have been given a new address, so print jobs find it but the scan software quietly points at the old one. Re-running the manufacturer's scan setup, or removing and re-adding the printer in your computer's settings, refreshes the registration and normally brings scanning back. It's bookkeeping rather than a fault — nothing needs repairing or replacing."}, {'q': 'Do I need to reinstall my printer after changing the WiFi password?', 'a': "Usually not. If only the password changed and the network name stayed the same, re-entering the password on the printer is normally enough and your computer carries on as before. The exception is when the printer comes back on the network with a new address — then the computer may still show it offline, and removing and re-adding the printer, or re-running the manufacturer's setup app, sorts it. Full driver reinstalls are rarely needed and mostly just cost you an evening."}, {'q': 'Can I use WPS to reconnect my printer without typing the password?', 'a': "Often, yes — if both your router and printer support it. Press the WPS button on the router, then within a couple of minutes press the WiFi or WPS button on the printer, and they exchange the new password automatically with no typing. Two honest caveats: some broadband routers have WPS switched off or removed for security reasons, and the exact button-press varies by printer, so it can take a couple of attempts. If WPS isn't available, the USB-cable-plus-app method is the reliable fallback."}, {'q': 'I changed my WiFi password after a scam call — was that the right thing to do?', 'a': "It's a sensible step, but on its own it isn't enough. Changing the WiFi password stops strangers using your network, but it doesn't remove any remote-access software a scammer had you install, and it doesn't protect your email or banking logins. Deal with those first: uninstall anything the caller had you download, change your important account passwords starting with email, then run a full malware scan and watch your bank statements for a couple of weeks. If you'd like a professional to check the machine over, that's a routine job for us."}], 'crossLinksHtml': '<p>Related help: <a href="/printer-support/">Printer support</a> · <a href="/printer-wont-connect-after-changing-broadband/">Printer won\'t connect after changing broadband</a> · <a href="/gave-a-scammer-remote-access/">Gave a scammer remote access?</a> · <a href="/remote-support/">Remote support</a></p>', 'primaryCta': ['Get it fixed remotely', '/remote-support/'], 'secondaryCta': ['Call 01202 775566', 'tel:+441202775566'], 'schemaKind': 'service'}, {'slug': 'printer-wont-connect-after-changing-broadband', 'title': "Printer Won't Connect After Changing Broadband | 365 Techies", 'metaDesc': "New broadband and now the printer won't connect? The rejoin steps, the static-IP trap, mesh and band-steering quirks — plain-English fixes from 365 Techies.", 'ogTitle': "Printer Won't Connect After Changing Broadband? Here's Why", 'crumbName': 'Printer After Broadband Switch', 'eyebrow': '// PRINTER & WI-FI HELP', 'h1': "Printer Won't Connect After Changing Broadband?", 'lede': "You've switched broadband, the new router is working, every phone and laptop is back online — and the printer is the one thing left behind. It's one of the most common problems after a provider switch, and it's almost never a broken printer. This guide walks through the fixes in order: rejoining the new Wi-Fi, the static-IP trap that strands printers, mesh and band-steering quirks, and when to reset and start clean.", 'chips': ['No fix, no fee', 'Family-run since 1995', '4.9★ rated on Google'], 'ctaHead': 'Printer still refusing to join the new network?', 'ctaSub': "We fix most of these in one short remote session — and if we can't fix it, you don't pay. Call 01202 775566.", 'serviceName': 'Printer & Network Support', 'sections': [{'eyebrow': '/01 &mdash; START HERE', 'h2': 'Why the printer got left behind when the broadband changed', 'html': "<p>You've switched broadband — new provider, new router, maybe a whole mesh kit — and everything reconnected except the printer. The laptops found the new Wi-Fi, the phones are fine, but the printer is sitting there saying offline, or has vanished from the list altogether. This is one of the most common calls we take in the week after a broadband switch, and it's almost never a broken printer.</p><p>Here's what actually happened: your printer memorised the old router's network name and password, and that network no longer exists. Even a replacement hub from the same provider broadcasts a new network. Some printers were also given a fixed address on the old network, and the new router may number its network from a different range entirely — which strands the printer even after it rejoins. The steps below apply whichever provider you've moved to or from; the box changes, but the pattern doesn't. In order, we'll cover:</p><ol><li>Rejoining the new Wi-Fi from the printer itself</li><li>Re-adding the printer on your computers</li><li>The stranded fixed-IP trap</li><li>Band steering, mesh and guest-network quirks</li><li>When to reset the printer's network settings and start clean</li></ol>"}, {'eyebrow': '/02 &mdash; THE QUICK FIXES', 'h2': 'Rejoin the new Wi-Fi, then re-add the printer', 'html': '<p>Start on the printer itself. Somewhere in its menus is a wireless or network setup option — the exact name moves between brands and models, so look for anything called <strong>wireless setup</strong>, <strong>network settings</strong> or <strong>Wi-Fi</strong>. Run it, choose your new network name (it\'s printed on a sticker on the router), and type the password exactly, capitals included. Screenless printers usually join through the maker\'s phone app or a WPS button press instead.</p><p>Once the printer is on the new network, don\'t assume your computers will find it. They often keep pointing at the printer\'s old entry, so remove the printer and add it again — or reinstall it with the manufacturer\'s app, which is usually the least fiddly route.</p><p>One genuinely useful shortcut: most provider hubs let you change their Wi-Fi name and password. Set the new router to broadcast the <em>same</em> name and password as the old one and the printer — along with everything else in the house — usually rejoins by itself. And if it was only the password that changed rather than the whole router, our guide to a <a href="/printer-stopped-working-after-changing-wifi-password/">printer that stopped working after a Wi-Fi password change</a> covers that exact case.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/03 &mdash; THE HIDDEN TRAP', 'h2': 'Stranded on a fixed IP: the 192.168.0.x vs 192.168.1.x problem', 'html': '<p>If you\'ve rejoined the Wi-Fi and the printer still won\'t talk to anything, this is usually why. Many printers — especially office models, or ones set up by an engineer — were given a fixed address on the old network, something like 192.168.1.50, so they could always be found in the same place. The catch is that your new router may number its network differently: one common range is 192.168.1.x, another is 192.168.0.x, and different makers favour different ones. A printer clinging to an address from the old range can join the new Wi-Fi, cheerfully report "connected", and still be unreachable — it\'s on the right network with the wrong postcode.</p><p>Two clues point to this: the printer\'s own test page prints fine while jobs from the computer never arrive, and the printer\'s address doesn\'t match the pattern of everything else on the network (most printers can print a network configuration page that shows it). The fix is to open the printer\'s network settings and put the address back to <strong>automatic</strong> — often labelled DHCP — or give it a new fixed address inside the new router\'s range. Then re-add the printer on your computers, because they\'ll likely still be aiming at the old address too.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/04 &mdash; MODERN HUBS', 'h2': 'Band steering, mesh systems and guest networks', 'html': '<p>New broadband often means a genuinely different kind of network, not just a different box. Most modern hubs and mesh systems broadcast one network name covering both the 2.4GHz and 5GHz bands, then quietly decide which band each device uses — a feature usually called <strong>band steering</strong> or smart connect. Most printers cope, but some older models only speak 2.4GHz and can fail repeatedly during setup if the hub keeps nudging them towards 5GHz. Setting the printer up close to the hub helps, and some routers let you split the bands into separately named networks while you get it joined — the option varies by hub, so don\'t worry if yours doesn\'t offer it.</p><p>If you\'ve gone from a single router to a mesh system, make sure the printer joined the mesh network and that the old router isn\'t still plugged in broadcasting its ghost — our <a href="/mesh-wifi-setup-guide/">mesh Wi-Fi setup guide</a> walks through getting every device onto the right network. And check the printer isn\'t on a <strong>guest network</strong>: guest networks deliberately wall devices off from each other, which is excellent for visitors and terrible for printing. For anything network-wide that\'s still misbehaving after the switch, our <a href="/wifi-troubleshooting/">Wi-Fi troubleshooting guide</a> covers the broader checks.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/05 &mdash; HONEST ADVICE', 'h2': 'When to reset — and when to stop fighting it yourself', 'html': '<p>If the rejoin, the re-add and the address check haven\'t sorted it, reset the printer\'s network settings and start clean. Most printers have an option to <strong>restore network defaults</strong> — separate from a full factory reset — which wipes only the saved Wi-Fi details and puts the printer back into setup mode. The name and location vary by brand, so look in the printer\'s network or setup menu. After the reset, run the wireless setup as if the printer were brand new, then re-add it on each computer.</p><p>A word of honesty: this problem almost never means you need a new printer, so don\'t let anyone talk you into one over a broadband switch. The machine is fine; it\'s just been left behind by a network that changed underneath it. The only time we\'d say stop fighting is when a very old printer no longer has working software for your version of Windows or macOS — and even then it\'s the drivers, not the broadband, making that decision.</p><p>If you\'ve been round the loop twice and it\'s still sulking, that\'s exactly what we\'re for. We fix most of these in a short remote session, no fix no fee — see our <a href="/printer-support/">printer support</a> service or call 01202 775566.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': "Why won't my printer connect after I switched broadband providers?", 'a': "Because your new router broadcasts a brand-new Wi-Fi network, and your printer is still looking for the old one. Printers memorise one network name and password, and when the old router goes, that network vanishes — even a replacement hub from the same provider creates a new network unless you rename it to match. The usual fix is to run the printer's wireless setup, join the new network, then re-add the printer on your computers. If it joins the Wi-Fi but still won't print, check whether it was given a fixed IP address on the old network, which strands it even after rejoining."}, {'q': 'Do I have to set up my printer again every time I get a new router?', 'a': "Usually yes — unless you give the new router the same Wi-Fi name and password as the old one. Most provider hubs let you change both in their settings or app; set them to match the old details and the printer, along with everything else in the house, normally rejoins by itself. Otherwise it's a one-off job per router: run the printer's wireless setup, choose the new network, enter the password carefully, and re-add the printer on each computer. It's tedious rather than technical, and once done it stays done until the next router change."}, {'q': "Why does my printer say it's connected but nothing will print?", 'a': "That usually means an address mismatch: the printer has joined the Wi-Fi but is holding an IP address from your old router's range, so your computers can't reach it. It also happens when a computer's printer entry is still pinned to the printer's old address. Set the printer's address back to automatic — often labelled DHCP — in its network settings, or give it a fixed address in the new router's range, then remove and re-add the printer on your computers. A printer sitting on a guest network shows the same symptom, because guest networks deliberately isolate devices from each other."}, {'q': "How do I reset my printer's network settings without wiping everything?", 'a': "Look for an option called something like restore network defaults or reset network settings — on most printers it's separate from a full factory reset and only clears the saved Wi-Fi details. Menu names move between brands and models, so check the network or setup section of the printer's own menus, or the maker's phone app. After the reset the printer behaves as though it has never been on Wi-Fi: run the wireless setup, join the new network, and re-add it on your computers. If you can't find the option, the manual for your exact model will name it."}, {'q': "Should I just buy a new printer if it won't connect to my new broadband?", 'a': "No — this is almost never a printer fault, and a new printer would need the same setup on the new network anyway. The printer stopped working because the network changed underneath it, not because it broke. The honest exception is a very old printer with no working software for your current version of Windows or macOS — that's worth weighing up, but it's a drivers problem, and a broadband switch alone isn't a reason to spend money. If you're stuck, a short remote session usually sorts it, and we don't charge if we can't fix it."}], 'crossLinksHtml': '<p><strong>Related guides:</strong> <a href="/printer-stopped-working-after-changing-wifi-password/">Printer stopped working after a Wi-Fi password change</a> · <a href="/mesh-wifi-setup-guide/">Mesh Wi-Fi setup guide</a> · <a href="/wifi-troubleshooting/">Wi-Fi troubleshooting</a> · <a href="/printer-support/">Printer support</a></p>', 'primaryCta': ['Get it fixed remotely', '/remote-support/'], 'secondaryCta': ['Call 01202 775566', 'tel:+441202775566'], 'schemaKind': 'service'}, {'slug': 'cctv-not-working-after-changing-broadband', 'title': 'CCTV Not Working After Changing Broadband? | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'Changed broadband and your CCTV went offline? Why a new router breaks remote viewing — port forwarding, static IPs, double NAT — and the safe way to fix it.', 'ogTitle': 'CCTV Stopped Working After a Broadband Switch? The Real Causes', 'crumbName': 'CCTV After Broadband Change', 'eyebrow': '// CCTV &AMP; BROADBAND', 'h1': "CCTV not working after changing broadband? Here's what actually broke", 'lede': "You switched broadband provider, the new router went in, and now your camera app says the system is offline — even though the cameras clearly have power. This is one of the most common calls we get after a broadband change, and it's almost never the cameras' fault. Here's why a new router silently breaks CCTV, how the fix differs for cloud and older port-forwarded systems, and the one thing we'd urge you not to do.", 'chips': ['Rated 4.9 on Google', 'No-fix-no-fee support', 'Family firm since 1995'], 'ctaHead': 'Cameras offline since the broadband switch?', 'ctaSub': 'One visit usually puts everything back — set up the secure way, not the risky way. Call 01202 775566 or book a visit.', 'serviceName': 'CCTV Reconfiguration After a Broadband Change', 'sections': [{'eyebrow': '/01 &mdash; THE SYMPTOM', 'h2': 'Everything worked until the new router arrived', 'html': '<p>The story is nearly always the same: the broadband switch went smoothly, the internet itself is fine, but the CCTV app now shows "offline", "connection failed" or an endlessly spinning wheel. The recorder may still show live pictures on its own monitor — proof the cameras are healthy — yet the moment you leave the house, nothing.</p><p>Nothing is broken. Your camera system was quietly configured around your old router, and the new one knows none of it. Four things cause almost every case of this:</p><ul><li><strong>Port-forwarding rules</strong> that lived inside the old router and went back in the box with it.</li><li><strong>A recorder with a fixed IP address</strong> that no longer matches the new router\'s network range.</li><li><strong>UPnP</strong> — the automatic version of port forwarding — switched off on the new hub.</li><li><strong>Double NAT</strong>, if a mesh WiFi system is now sitting behind the provider\'s hub.</li></ul><p>Which of these applies, and what the right fix is, depends on whether yours is a modern cloud/P2P system or an older port-forwarded DVR — they fail for different reasons and need different repairs. We\'ll take them in turn, including the honest security question most guides skip.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/02 &mdash; WHAT BROKE', 'h2': 'The settings that died with your old router', 'html': '<p>Port forwarding is the classic culprit. Older CCTV installs work by opening a "door" in the router that lets your phone reach the DVR or NVR from outside. Those rules live inside the router itself, so when the old one was unplugged, the rules went with it. The new router arrives with no doors open at all — and remote viewing dies instantly.</p><p>The second, sneakier problem is the recorder\'s own address. Installers often give a DVR a fixed (static) IP so the forwarding rule always points at the right box. If it was set to something like 192.168.1.100 but your new provider\'s hub uses a different range, the recorder is now stranded on a network that no longer exists — it can\'t even reach the internet, let alone your phone. Address ranges vary between providers, which is exactly why a swap that "should just work" often doesn\'t.</p><p>Finally, some systems relied on UPnP to open ports automatically. Plenty of current hubs ship with it disabled — and, in fairness, for sound security reasons, which we\'ll come back to below. The result is the same either way: the path from your phone to your recorder no longer exists. One more modern wrinkle: some newer providers — most 4G and 5G home broadband, Starlink&rsquo;s standard plan, and a number of full-fibre networks — put you behind carrier-grade NAT, where you share a public address with other customers. Behind CGNAT, port forwarding simply cannot work no matter what you set, and the honest fixes are the camera maker&rsquo;s cloud/P2P service, a VPN, or asking the provider whether a public IP is available.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/03 &mdash; TWO KINDS OF SYSTEM', 'h2': 'Cloud (P2P) cameras and old-school DVRs need different fixes', 'html': '<p>Modern systems — Ring, Eufy, Tapo, and most recent Hikvision or Dahua kits used through the maker\'s own app — are "P2P" or cloud-connected. The recorder or camera phones home to the vendor\'s servers and your app meets it there, so no port forwarding is needed. After a broadband change these usually fail for one simple reason: the kit can\'t get online. Wired recorders may need their network settings moved onto the new range; WiFi cameras need the new network\'s name and password. A tip worth knowing before your next switch: if you set the new router\'s WiFi name and password to match the old ones, most wireless cameras reconnect on their own.</p><p>Older port-forwarded DVR systems — often reached via an IP address or DDNS name typed into the app — are a different job. Getting them going again means giving the recorder a valid address on the new network, then deciding how to make it reachable from outside, which is where the security question below really matters.</p><p>If your cameras sit in an outbuilding, there\'s an extra wrinkle — the link back to the house has to survive the router change too. Our guide to <a href="/cctv-in-detached-garage-connect-to-house/">connecting garage CCTV to the house</a> covers that side.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/04 &mdash; THE HIDDEN CULPRIT', 'h2': 'Mesh WiFi and double NAT', 'html': '<p>A broadband change is often the moment people add mesh WiFi — and mesh is behind a surprising share of these callouts. If the mesh system is plugged into the provider\'s hub and both are acting as routers, you now have two networks stacked on top of each other: "double NAT". A port-forwarding rule on one router doesn\'t carry through the other, so remote viewing fails even if you configure one of them perfectly. Some cloud systems cope with double NAT; others become flaky or drop off entirely.</p><p>The clean fix is to have only one device doing the routing — usually by putting the provider\'s hub into modem or bridge mode so the mesh takes over. The setting\'s name and availability vary by provider, and some hubs don\'t offer it at all, in which case workarounds like an IP passthrough or DMZ setting pointed at the mesh can do the job — but they need doing carefully, or you trade one problem for another.</p><p>This is bread-and-butter work for us on <a href="/business-wifi-installation/">business WiFi installations</a>, where cameras, tills and door systems all have to survive a network change together — and exactly the same thinking applies to a home setup.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/05 &mdash; OUR HONEST ADVICE', 'h2': "When we'd tell you not to rebuild the old setup", 'html': '<p>Here\'s the part many guides skip: if your old router had port-forwarding rules pointing at a DVR, we\'d think twice before recreating them. A forwarded port puts the recorder directly on the public internet, where automated scanners find it within hours. That was normal practice a decade ago; today, older DVRs that no longer receive firmware updates are among the most commonly hijacked devices in a home. If your recorder is ancient and unsupported, the honest answer may be that it shouldn\'t be internet-facing at all.</p><p>What we\'d do instead, in order of preference: use the maker\'s cloud/P2P app if the recorder supports it; set up a VPN into your home network so only you can reach the recorder; and treat port forwarding as a last resort, on current, updated kit with strong passwords. Sometimes the money is genuinely better spent on a modern system than on wrestling an old one back online — our <a href="/cctv-smart-home/">CCTV and smart home page</a> covers what that looks like.</p><p>We do on-site visits across Bournemouth, Poole and the surrounding area to reconfigure CCTV after a broadband change, done the secure way. And if a rebuild isn\'t worth it, we\'ll say so before you spend a penny — <a href="/contact/">get in touch</a> or call 01202 775566.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Why did my CCTV stop working when I changed broadband provider?', 'a': "Because the settings your CCTV depended on lived inside the old router, and the new one doesn't have them. Port-forwarding rules are wiped when the router changes, the recorder's fixed IP address may no longer match the new router's network range, and features like UPnP are often switched off on new hubs. The cameras and recorder are usually fine — it's the network around them that changed. Cloud (P2P) systems typically just need to get back online; older port-forwarded DVRs need proper reconfiguration, ideally done a more secure way than before."}, {'q': 'Do I need to set up port forwarding again on my new router?', 'a': "Possibly — but check first whether your system actually needs it, because many don't. Modern cloud or P2P systems (Ring, Eufy, and most recent Hikvision or Dahua kits used through the maker's app) connect outwards to the vendor's servers and need no port forwarding at all; they just need the recorder or cameras back online. Only older systems reached by IP address or DDNS relied on forwarded ports. If yours is one of those, we'd honestly steer you towards the vendor's app or a VPN instead where possible, because an internet-exposed DVR is a real security risk."}, {'q': 'Is it safe to port forward my DVR to the internet?', 'a': "It's riskier than it used to be, and with an old DVR we'd say no. A forwarded port makes the recorder directly reachable from anywhere, and automated scanners probe for exposed DVRs constantly. Recorders that still receive firmware updates and have strong passwords lower the risk; older, unsupported units with known flaws are a genuine hazard and get hijacked regularly. Safer options are the maker's cloud/P2P app, which needs no open ports, or a VPN into your network. If port forwarding truly is the only option, it should be a deliberate, last-resort choice on up-to-date kit."}, {'q': "Why can I see my cameras at home but not when I'm out?", 'a': "That pattern almost always means the cameras are healthy but unreachable from outside your network. At home, your phone finds the recorder directly on the local network; away from home, it has to come in via the internet — which is exactly the path a router change breaks. The usual causes are missing port-forwarding rules on the new router, a recorder that can't get online because its fixed IP no longer matches the new network, or double NAT if a mesh WiFi system now sits behind the provider's hub. Fix the recorder's internet access first, then the remote path."}, {'q': 'Will my wireless cameras reconnect to the new router by themselves?', 'a': "Only if the new WiFi has exactly the same name and password as the old one. Cameras store the network name (SSID) and password, so if either has changed — which it almost always has with a new provider's hub — each camera needs the new details, usually via its app's setup or reconnect option. A useful trick before your next switch: rename the new router's WiFi to match the old name and password, and most wireless cameras — along with smart plugs and doorbells — come back on their own without being touched."}, {'q': 'Can someone come out and fix my CCTV after a broadband change?', 'a': "Yes — this is a job we do regularly across Bournemouth, Poole and the surrounding area. We'll get the recorder back onto the new network, reconnect any wireless cameras, sort out mesh and double-NAT tangles, and set up remote viewing the secure way — vendor app or VPN rather than a bare open port wherever possible. If we find the recorder is too old to be safely put online, we'll tell you straight and talk through the options rather than quietly recreating a risky setup. Call 01202 775566 or use our contact page to arrange a visit."}], 'crossLinksHtml': '<p>Related: <a href="/cctv-smart-home/">CCTV &amp; smart home installation</a> · <a href="/cctv-in-detached-garage-connect-to-house/">Connecting garage CCTV to the house</a> · <a href="/business-wifi-installation/">Business WiFi installation</a> · <a href="/contact/">Contact 365 Techies</a></p>', 'primaryCta': ['Book a visit', '/contact/'], 'secondaryCta': ['Call 01202 775566', 'tel:+441202775566'], 'schemaKind': 'service'}, {'slug': 'printer-prints-but-wont-scan-after-new-router', 'title': "Printer Prints but Won't Scan After New Router | 365 Techies", 'metaDesc': "Printer prints but won't scan after a new router? Why it happens, the settings to check, and the fix that usually works — from 365 Techies, Bournemouth.", 'ogTitle': "Printer Prints but Won't Scan After a New Router? The Fix", 'crumbName': "Prints but Won't Scan", 'eyebrow': '// PRINTER & SCANNER HELP', 'h1': "Printer Prints but Won't Scan After a New Router?", 'lede': "A new router arrives, printing carries on as if nothing happened — but scanning to the computer dies. It's one of the most common printer calls we take, and it isn't your printer failing. Scanning needs the printer to find your computer across the network, and new routers often block exactly that. Here's how to check it, how to fix it, and a scan method that is far harder to break.", 'chips': ['No-fix-no-fee remote help', 'Rated 4.9 on Google', 'Family-run since 1995'], 'ctaHead': "Still won't scan? Let us take a look.", 'ctaSub': 'We fix most printer and scanner faults remotely in a single session — no fix, no fee. Call 01202 775566 or book remote support.', 'serviceName': 'Printer & Scanner Support', 'sections': [{'eyebrow': '/01 &mdash; THE SYMPTOM', 'h2': "Prints fine, won't scan — why the new router breaks only half the machine", 'html': '<p>You\'ve had a new router — perhaps with a broadband switch — and now the printer happily prints everything you send it, but scanning to the computer has stopped dead. The scan button errors, says no computer was found, or the scanner software insists the printer is offline. It feels absurd: how can half the machine be connected?</p><p>The answer is that printing and scanning travel in opposite directions. When you print, your <strong>computer finds the printer</strong>. When you press Scan on the printer, the <strong>printer has to find your computer</strong> — and it does that using network "discovery" chatter that new routers frequently block, or that breaks when the new router hands out different addresses than the old one did. Below we\'ll check you\'re genuinely on one network, fix the settings that quietly block discovery, re-register the scanner properly on the computer, and — if all else fails — set up a scan method that doesn\'t care what your router does.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/02 &mdash; FIRST CHECK', 'h2': 'Make sure both devices are really on the same network', 'html': '<p>Before touching drivers, confirm the printer and computer are genuinely on the same network — after a router swap they often aren\'t, even when both say "connected". Most printers can print a <strong>network configuration report</strong> from their own menu (usually somewhere under network or report settings — the wording varies by brand). Compare the printer\'s IP address with your computer\'s: on a typical home network the first three groups of numbers should match, something like 192.168.1.x on both. If they don\'t match, the two devices are effectively on different networks and scan-to-computer cannot work until that\'s fixed.</p><p>The common culprits: the printer quietly joined the router\'s <strong>guest network</strong> (guest networks deliberately wall devices off from each other), a mesh system placed the two on different segments, or the printer is still clinging to the old router\'s WiFi if that box is still powered up in a corner. If the WiFi itself has been odd since the swap, our <a href="/wifi-troubleshooting/">WiFi troubleshooting guide</a> covers the wider picture.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/03 &mdash; HIDDEN BLOCKERS', 'h2': 'Two settings that silently block scanning', 'html': '<p>Two settings cause most of these cases, and neither produces a helpful error message.</p><p><strong>First, isolation on the router.</strong> Many new routers ship with a setting called AP isolation, client isolation or WiFi isolation — sometimes it\'s simply how the guest network behaves. It stops wireless devices talking to each other, which is exactly what scan-to-computer needs to do. Log into the router\'s settings and look for that wording; where it lives differs by make and model, so we\'d rather name the setting than invent a menu path. While you\'re there, make sure the printer isn\'t sitting on the guest network.</p><p><strong>Second, the Windows firewall profile.</strong> When your computer meets a new router, Windows treats it as a brand-new network and often marks it "Public" — a cautious mode that blocks incoming connections, including the printer announcing it has a scan for you. In your network settings, set your home network to <strong>Private</strong> (the exact wording moves between Windows versions). That one change fixes a surprising share of these calls on its own.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/04 &mdash; THE RELIABLE FIX', 'h2': 'Remove the printer and reinstall the full software package', 'html': '<p>If the network checks pass and it still won\'t scan, the problem is usually stale plumbing on the computer. Windows and the printer\'s own software register the scanning components (the WIA and TWAIN pieces that scanner apps rely on) against the printer\'s old address on the old router. Printing survives because Windows re-finds printers fairly gracefully; the scan registrations just quietly rot.</p><p>The clean fix: remove the printer from your computer entirely, then download the <strong>full software package</strong> from the manufacturer\'s website — not just the bare driver Windows offers automatically — and run it fresh. The full package redoes the scan registrations and re-points "scan to computer" at your machine\'s current address. It takes about ten minutes and it\'s the single most reliable cure we know for this fault.</p><p>If printing has <em>also</em> stopped since the router change, that\'s a related but different fault — our guide to a <a href="/printer-wont-connect-after-changing-broadband/">printer that won\'t connect after changing broadband</a> walks through that one step by step.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/05 &mdash; THE STURDY WORKAROUND', 'h2': 'Scan to email: the method that ignores your router', 'html': "<p>There's a version of scanning that simply doesn't care what your router blocks: <strong>scan to email</strong>. Set up on the printer itself, it sends each scan as an email attachment, so the printer never has to find your computer at all. Once configured it survives router swaps, new laptops and network changes untouched — plenty of our customers switch to it permanently after one of these episodes. Most modern multifunction printers support it, though setup varies by brand and some need an email account's app-password before they can send.</p><p>Two more sturdy alternatives. <strong>Scan to a USB stick</strong>, if the printer has a port, needs no network at all — walk the stick to the computer. And driving the scan <em>from the computer</em> — opening the scanner app on your PC and starting the scan there, rather than pressing the button on the printer — reverses the direction of the conversation, and often works even while the printer's own scan button is still failing. One honest caveat: scan-to-email survives router swaps only when it is set up with a provider-independent account (a Gmail or Outlook.com address, say). If it was configured against your old broadband provider&rsquo;s email servers, changing provider breaks it too — re-point it at an independent account and it stays fixed.</p>"}, {'eyebrow': '/06 &mdash; HONEST ADVICE', 'h2': "When it's not worth fighting — and what we'd do", 'html': '<p>Honest advice: don\'t buy a new printer for this. This fault is almost never the printer dying — it\'s configuration, and it\'s fixable. Equally, don\'t burn a whole weekend on it. If you\'ve checked the network, turned isolation off, reinstalled the full package and it still refuses, the sensible ceiling is about an hour of your own time.</p><p>The one exception is a very old printer whose manufacturer software no longer supports current versions of Windows. If the maker\'s website has no package for your Windows version, you can be endlessly patient and still lose — scan-to-email or a USB stick may be the permanent answer, or, if scanning matters to you daily, a modest replacement can genuinely be cheaper than the hours.</p><p>And if this guide hasn\'t sorted it, that\'s exactly what we\'re for. We fix most printer and scanner faults by <a href="/remote-support/">remote support</a> on a no-fix-no-fee basis — see our <a href="/printer-support/">printer support</a> page, or call us on 01202 775566 and we\'ll tell you honestly whether it\'s worth pursuing.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Why does my printer print but not scan after getting a new router?', 'a': 'Because printing and scanning travel in opposite directions: your computer finds the printer to print, but the printer has to find your computer to scan, and that discovery step is what new routers break. Fresh routers often block device-to-device chatter (a setting called AP or client isolation), hand out different addresses, or land the printer on a guest network that walls devices off from each other. Meanwhile Windows may treat the new router as an unknown "Public" network and block the incoming scan. The printer itself is almost certainly fine — this is configuration, not hardware.'}, {'q': 'How do I get my printer to scan to my computer again?', 'a': "The most reliable fix is to remove the printer from your computer, then download and run the manufacturer's full software package — not just the basic driver Windows installs on its own. The full package re-registers the scanning components against the printer's new network address, which is the part that actually broke. Before that, confirm both devices are on the same network (not the guest WiFi), check the router isn't set to isolate wireless devices from each other, and make sure Windows treats your home network as Private rather than Public so incoming scans aren't blocked."}, {'q': 'What is AP isolation and can it stop my printer scanning?', 'a': "AP isolation — also called client isolation or WiFi isolation — is a router setting that stops wireless devices on the same network talking to each other, and yes, it stops scan-to-computer completely. It exists for security and is standard behaviour on guest networks, but it blocks exactly the conversation a scan needs: the printer contacting your computer. Printing can still work because that connection runs the other way. Log into your router's settings and look for that wording; where it lives varies by make. Also check the printer hasn't joined the guest network, which behaves the same way."}, {'q': 'Is scan to email better than scanning to my computer?', 'a': "For a lot of people, yes — scan to email is set up on the printer itself and sends each scan as an email attachment, so the printer never needs to find your computer and router changes can't break it. Once it's working, it survives new routers, new laptops and Windows updates untouched, which is why many of our customers keep it permanently after one of these faults. The trade-offs: setup varies by brand, some printers need an email app-password before they can send, and very large multi-page scans can bump into attachment size limits."}, {'q': 'Do I need a new printer if it stopped scanning after a router change?', 'a': "Almost certainly not — this fault is configuration, not a dying printer, and it's usually fixable in under an hour. The one honest exception is a very old printer whose manufacturer no longer publishes software for current versions of Windows: without that full package the scan components can't be re-registered properly, and scan-to-email or scanning to a USB stick may be the practical long-term answer. If scanning matters to you daily and the software genuinely no longer exists, a modest replacement can cost less than the hours you'd spend fighting it."}], 'crossLinksHtml': '<p>Related help: <a href="/printer-support/">Printer support</a> · <a href="/printer-wont-connect-after-changing-broadband/">Printer won\'t connect after changing broadband</a> · <a href="/wifi-troubleshooting/">WiFi troubleshooting</a> · <a href="/remote-support/">Remote support</a></p>', 'primaryCta': ['Get it fixed remotely', '/remote-support/'], 'secondaryCta': ['Call 01202 775566', 'tel:+441202775566'], 'schemaKind': 'service'}, {'slug': 'emails-on-computer-but-not-phone', 'title': 'Emails on Computer but Not Phone? 3 Checks | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'Emails on your computer but not your phone? Three quick checks find the cause — POP vs IMAP, password changes, sync settings — plus plain-English fixes.', 'ogTitle': 'Emails on Your Computer but Not Your Phone? The 4 Causes', 'crumbName': 'Emails Not on Phone', 'eyebrow': '// EMAIL TROUBLESHOOTING', 'h1': 'Getting emails on your computer but not your phone?', 'lede': "You can see new emails on the computer, but your phone shows nothing — or stopped dead weeks ago — even though it's the same address. It's one of the most common email calls we take, and it almost always comes down to one of four causes. Three quick checks will tell you which one you've got, and the fix for each is below — in plain English, no jargon.", 'chips': ['Trading since 1995', '4.9★ on Google', 'No fix, no fee'], 'ctaHead': 'Phone still not getting your emails?', 'ctaSub': "We'll connect securely, find the cause, and set the account up properly on every device. No fix, no fee — call 01202 775566.", 'serviceName': 'Email Sync Fix', 'sections': [{'eyebrow': '/01 &mdash; THE SYMPTOM', 'h2': 'Same account, but the phone shows nothing', 'html': "<p>New emails land on the computer all day long, but your phone's inbox sits empty — or it stopped at some date weeks ago and never moved again. Same email address, same account, two very different results. If that's you, you're in the right place.</p><p>The good news is this problem is rarely mysterious. In our experience it almost always comes down to one of four causes:</p><ul><li><strong>POP vs IMAP</strong> — the computer is quietly taking the mail off the server before the phone ever sees it.</li><li><strong>A password or security change</strong> that the computer survived and the phone didn't.</li><li><strong>Sync switched off</strong> — often by a battery saver you never touched.</li><li><strong>A folder-view difference</strong> — the mail is on the phone, just not where you're looking.</li></ul><p>Below are three quick checks that tell you which cause you've got, then the fix for each. None of them needs any technical knowledge, and none of them can make things worse.</p>"}, {'eyebrow': '/02 &mdash; DIAGNOSE IT', 'h2': 'Three checks that find the cause in minutes', 'html': '<p>Before changing any settings, do these three checks in order — each one rules causes in or out.</p><ol><li><strong>Check webmail in a browser.</strong> On any device, sign in to your email through the website (gmail.com, outlook.com, or your provider\'s webmail page). Are the "missing" emails there? <em>If they\'re not on the webmail either</em>, your computer is almost certainly using POP and removing mail from the server — jump to the POP section below. <em>If they are there</em>, the server is fine and the problem is on the phone.</li><li><strong>Open the phone\'s mail app and drag down to refresh.</strong> Watch for an error message, a "cannot connect" banner, or a prompt asking for your password. A password prompt — or a quiet notification about the account needing attention — points to the security-change cause.</li><li><strong>Search the phone for a recent email you know exists.</strong> If search finds it but your inbox doesn\'t show it, nothing is actually broken — it\'s a folder or filtered-inbox view issue, which is the easiest fix of all.</li></ol><p>Whichever check lights up, the matching fix is in the sections below.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/03 &mdash; CAUSE 1', 'h2': 'POP on the computer is taking the mail first', 'html': '<p>Older email setups often use <strong>POP</strong> (Post Office Protocol). POP works like the paper post: the computer collects each message and — depending on its settings — <strong>removes it from the server</strong>. Once that has happened, your phone connects, finds an empty mailbox, and shows you nothing. Nothing is broken; the computer simply got there first.</p><p><strong>IMAP</strong>, the modern alternative, leaves everything on the server and keeps every device in step — read a message on the computer and it shows as read on the phone too.</p><p>The fix has two parts. First, stop the leak: in the computer\'s email account settings, find the option worded something like <em>"leave a copy of messages on the server"</em> and turn it on (the exact wording moves around between programs and versions). Second, for the proper long-term fix, set the account up as IMAP — or Exchange/Microsoft 365 where your provider offers it — on both devices. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-set-up-email-on-your-phone/">setting up email on your phone</a> walks through it gently.</p><p>One honest warning: emails POP has already pulled off the server now exist <em>only</em> on that computer. The phone can\'t reach back in time for them — so don\'t delete the computer\'s email profile in a fit of tidying.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/04 &mdash; CAUSE 2', 'h2': 'A password or 2FA change broke the phone quietly', 'html': '<p>Changed your email password recently? Turned on two-step verification? Had a security update forced on you by your provider? Any of these can break email on one device while the other carries on. Computers often keep working for a while on a saved sign-in, so the phone fails first — and phones are terrible at telling you. Many just stop syncing silently, or tuck an "account action required" notice away where nobody looks.</p><p>The fix is usually simple: open the phone\'s mail settings for that account and re-enter the password. If it still refuses, two wrinkles are worth knowing:</p><ul><li><strong>App passwords.</strong> With two-step verification on, some providers won\'t accept your normal password in older mail apps — they issue a separate "app password" from your account\'s security page instead. Providers have been steadily retiring these in favour of proper sign-in screens, so what your account offers varies.</li><li><strong>Microsoft accounts have their own quirks.</strong> Sign-in methods for Outlook, Hotmail and Microsoft 365 addresses have moved around a lot in recent years; if it\'s one of those that won\'t behave, our <a href="/outlook-problems/">Outlook problems guide</a> covers the common snags.</li></ul><p>If re-entering the password puts you in a loop, removing and re-adding the account on the phone often clears it — and that\'s safe with IMAP, because your mail lives on the server, not the phone.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/05 &mdash; CAUSES 3 & 4', 'h2': 'Sync switched off, battery savers, and folder tricks', 'html': '<p>If webmail shows your emails and the password is fine, the phone itself is usually suppressing sync — or hiding the mail in plain sight.</p><ul><li><strong>Sync toggled off.</strong> Both Android and iPhone have per-account switches controlling whether mail fetches automatically. They\'re easy to knock off by accident, and some "phone cleaner" apps switch them off on purpose. Look in the phone\'s mail or accounts settings for the account and check sync is on.</li><li><strong>Battery savers.</strong> Power-saving modes — especially on Android — stop apps working in the background to stretch the battery, and mail apps are a favourite victim. If email only appears when you open the app and refresh by hand, this is the classic sign. Exempt the mail app from battery optimisation, or turn the saver off and see if mail starts flowing again.</li><li><strong>Fetch set to manual (iPhone).</strong> iPhones can be set to check mail on a schedule, or only when you open the app. If new mail appears only on opening, check the Fetch/Push setting for that account.</li><li><strong>Folder views.</strong> Focused inboxes, "All Mail" versus "Inbox", and overzealous spam filters all hide mail that has genuinely arrived. If search found the message in check three, look at which folder it\'s sitting in — then adjust the view or the filter, not the account.</li></ul>'}, {'eyebrow': '/06 &mdash; HONEST ADVICE', 'h2': 'When to stop fighting the built-in mail app', 'html': '<p>Here\'s the advice we give our own family: <strong>the phone\'s built-in mail app is often the least reliable link in this whole chain.</strong> Provider-made apps — the Gmail app for Gmail addresses, the Outlook app for Microsoft addresses — handle passwords, two-step verification and background sync far more gracefully, because the people who run the mail service also wrote the app. If your built-in app keeps dropping the account, installing the provider\'s own free app and signing in is frequently the five-minute fix that ends the saga. It costs nothing and your emails stay exactly where they are.</p><p>And know when to hand it over. If you\'ve done the three checks, tried the matching fix, and the phone still won\'t sync — or the word "POP" has just revealed years of email trapped on one ageing computer — that\'s exactly what we\'re for. We connect securely over <a href="/remote-support/">remote support</a>, sort the account out properly on every device, and move old mail somewhere safe first. No fix, no fee, and we\'ll tell you honestly if the cheapest answer is a free app rather than an hour of our time. Our <a href="/email-support/">email support service</a> covers all of this — call 01202 775566.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Why am I getting emails on my computer but not on my phone?', 'a': "Usually because the computer is collecting mail with POP and removing it from the server before your phone checks, or because a password or security change broke the phone's connection without telling you. The quickest test is signing in to your email through a web browser: if the missing messages aren't there either, the computer is taking them (that's POP); if they are there, the fault is on the phone — most often a wrong saved password, sync switched off, or a battery saver stopping the mail app working in the background."}, {'q': "What's the difference between POP and IMAP email?", 'a': 'POP downloads each email to one device and, depending on its settings, deletes it from the server — so other devices never see it. IMAP keeps everything on the server and mirrors it to every device, so your phone, tablet and computer always match. IMAP is the right choice for almost everyone today; POP made sense when households had one computer and dial-up internet. If your account is still on POP, the lasting fix is to set it up again as IMAP — or Exchange/Microsoft 365 where offered — on all your devices.'}, {'q': 'Why did my phone stop getting emails after I changed my password?', 'a': 'Because the phone is still trying the old password, and many phones fail silently rather than asking for the new one. Open the phone\'s mail or accounts settings, find the email account and re-enter the password there. If it still won\'t connect and you use two-step verification, your provider may want an "app password" for older mail apps — created on your account\'s security page — though providers are steadily phasing these out. If you end up in a sign-in loop, removing and re-adding the account usually clears it, and with IMAP your mail stays safe on the server.'}, {'q': 'How do I know if my email is set up as POP or IMAP?', 'a': "Look in your email program's account settings, where the incoming server type is shown as POP (sometimes POP3) or IMAP — the exact location varies between programs and versions, but it's usually under account or server settings. There's also a practical tell: with IMAP, reading or deleting a message on one device changes it everywhere; with POP, each device keeps its own separate copy and they drift apart. And if emails vanish from webmail shortly after your computer collects them, that's POP set to remove mail from the server."}, {'q': 'Will I lose my emails if I delete the account from my phone and add it again?', 'a': "With IMAP or Exchange, no — your emails live on the provider's server, and re-adding the account simply downloads them again. Removing an account from a phone doesn't delete anything from the server. The one real risk is POP: messages already pulled down to a computer may exist only on that computer, so never delete the email profile or its data files on the computer itself without checking first. If you're not sure which type you have, check before deleting anything — or ask us and we'll tell you in a minute."}, {'q': "Is the Gmail or Outlook app better than my phone's built-in mail app?", 'a': "For reliability, usually yes. The provider's own app — Gmail for Google addresses, Outlook for Microsoft addresses — is written by the same people who run the mail service, so it handles sign-in, two-step verification and background sync with far fewer tantrums than many built-in mail apps. Built-in apps are fine when they work, but when one keeps dropping an account, we'd rather install the free official app than spend an hour fighting settings. Your email address and messages stay exactly the same whichever app you read them in."}], 'crossLinksHtml': '<p><strong>More email help:</strong> <a href="/how-to-set-up-email-on-your-phone/">Set up email on your phone</a> · <a href="/outlook-problems/">Outlook problems</a> · <a href="/email-support/">Email support</a> · <a href="/remote-support/">How remote support works</a></p>', 'primaryCta': ['Get it fixed remotely', '/remote-support/'], 'secondaryCta': ['Call 01202 775566', 'tel:+441202775566'], 'schemaKind': 'service'}, {'slug': 'new-outlook-emails-stuck-in-drafts', 'title': 'New Outlook Emails Stuck in Drafts – Fixes | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'Emails vanishing into Drafts in new Outlook? Why the app skips the Outbox, the checks and fixes that work — and how to go back to classic Outlook.', 'ogTitle': 'New Outlook Emails Stuck in Drafts? Why It Happens and the Fixes', 'crumbName': 'Stuck in Drafts (new Outlook)', 'eyebrow': '// OUTLOOK TROUBLESHOOTING', 'h1': 'New Outlook: emails stuck in Drafts and not sending', 'lede': "New Outlook has a habit that catches almost everyone out: when an email fails to send, it doesn't sit in an Outbox with a warning — it slips silently back into Drafts. Here's why the new app behaves this way, the checks and fixes that actually help, and the honest alternative many people choose: classic Outlook still exists, and going back is allowed.", 'chips': ['No fix, no fee', 'Family-run since 1995', 'Rated 4.9 on Google'], 'ctaHead': 'Still watching emails slip into Drafts?', 'ctaSub': 'We fix Outlook remotely, often the same day — no fix, no fee. Call 01202 775566 and talk to a real person in Bournemouth.', 'serviceName': 'Outlook Email Sending Fix', 'sections': [{'eyebrow': '/01 &mdash; THE SYMPTOM', 'h2': 'You pressed Send — and found it in Drafts days later', 'html': '<p>You typed the email, pressed Send, and it never arrived. No error, no bounce — then you find it sitting in Drafts as though you never sent it at all. If that\'s what\'s happening, you\'re almost certainly using the <strong>new Outlook</strong>: the app Microsoft is rolling out to replace Windows Mail and, in time, classic Outlook. This quiet parking-in-Drafts habit is one of the most common complaints we hear about it.</p><p>One important distinction before we start: if you\'re on <em>classic</em> Outlook and messages are piling up in an Outbox folder, that\'s a different fault with different fixes — see our guide to <a href="/outlook-stuck-in-outbox/">emails stuck in the Outbox</a> instead. On this page we\'ll cover why new Outlook behaves this way, the quick checks worth running first, the fixes that usually get mail moving again, and the honest option many people choose in the end: going back to classic Outlook.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/02 &mdash; THE CAUSE', 'h2': 'Why new Outlook parks unsent mail in Drafts', 'html': "<p>Classic Outlook and the old Mail app talked to your mail server directly: your PC connected, handed the message over, and anything still waiting sat visibly in an Outbox. New Outlook works differently for Gmail, IMAP and most other non-Microsoft accounts: it routes them through Microsoft's cloud, which syncs with your provider on your behalf.</p><p>That design has consequences. For many account types there is <strong>no traditional Outbox at all</strong> — and when a send fails, whether because the connection dropped, your sign-in needed refreshing, the server refused the message or an attachment was too large, the app often has nowhere to queue it. So it tucks the message back into Drafts, frequently without any visible error. Nothing is lost — the email is safe — but nothing is sent either, and unless you go looking you may not notice for days.</p><p>To be fair to Microsoft, new Outlook is updated constantly and behaviour genuinely varies between versions and account types, so we won't pretend every copy acts identically. But if your unsent mail keeps surfacing in Drafts, this sync model is almost certainly why.</p>"}, {'eyebrow': '/03 &mdash; QUICK CHECKS', 'h2': 'Five checks before you change anything', 'html': '<p>Run these in order — they take five minutes and often find the culprit.</p><ol><li><strong>Confirm it really didn\'t send.</strong> Check your Sent folder, then log into your provider\'s webmail. Occasionally the message did go and a stray copy stayed in Drafts — chasing a fault that doesn\'t exist wastes an afternoon.</li><li><strong>Check the account is syncing at all.</strong> Is new mail arriving? Look for a sign-in or sync warning near the account name — an expired sign-in silently stops sending too.</li><li><strong>Suspect the attachment.</strong> Most providers cap messages somewhere around 20–25&nbsp;MB, and limits vary. Try resending without the attachment; if it flies, share the file another way.</li><li><strong>Re-check the recipient\'s address.</strong> A mistyped address can cause a rejection the app doesn\'t clearly surface.</li><li><strong>Send yourself a plain one-line test email.</strong> If even that won\'t go, the problem is the account or the app rather than one message — our guide to <a href="/outlook-not-sending-emails/">Outlook not sending emails</a> covers the wider possibilities.</li></ol>'}, {'eyebrow': '/04 &mdash; THE FIXES', 'h2': 'The fixes that usually get mail moving', 'html': "<p>Start gentle and escalate:</p><ul><li><strong>Resend from Drafts.</strong> Open the stuck message and press Send again. A one-off sync hiccup is the most common cause, and a simple resend clears a surprising number of these.</li><li><strong>Restart and update the app.</strong> Close new Outlook fully, then check the Microsoft Store for updates. The app changes almost monthly, and sending bugs do get fixed.</li><li><strong>Sign out and back in.</strong> A stale sign-in stops sending without a clear error. Look for the option in the app's account settings.</li><li><strong>Remove and re-add the account.</strong> This forces a completely fresh sync and sorts the stubborn cases. You'll find it in the app's settings under <em>Accounts</em> — menu names move between versions, so look for the Accounts section rather than following a remembered click-path. First, copy the text of any stuck draft into Notepad or a document — a draft that failed to sync may exist only on this PC. For IMAP and Microsoft accounts your delivered mail lives on the server, so re-adding the account brings it back, though we'd still note your settings down first.</li><li><strong>Use webmail as a stopgap.</strong> If webmail sends fine, your account is healthy and the fault is the app — genuinely useful to know, and it keeps you working in the meantime.</li></ul><p>We can't promise any one of these will fix it — but between them they resolve the great majority of cases we see.</p>"}, {'eyebrow': '/05 &mdash; HONEST ADVICE', 'h2': "When it's not worth fighting — and what we'd do", 'html': '<p>Here\'s the honest bit. New Outlook is still a young app, and this Drafts behaviour has rumbled on through many versions. If email matters to your working day, we wouldn\'t spend hours re-adding accounts and hoping. <strong>Classic Outlook still exists</strong>, still has a proper Outbox that shows you exactly what hasn\'t gone, and talks to your mail server directly. Plenty of our customers have simply switched back and been happier for it — our guide on <a href="/how-to-go-back-to-classic-outlook/">how to go back to classic Outlook</a> walks through it step by step. One honest caveat before you head that way: classic Outlook needs a Microsoft 365 subscription or an Office licence that includes it — if new Outlook is all you\'ve ever had (say, where it replaced the old Mail app), there may be no classic Outlook to go back to, and we\'ll talk you through the options instead.</p><p>If you\'d rather stay on the new app, keep webmail open as a safety net and glance at Drafts before you finish for the day, so nothing can hide there for a week.</p><p>And if none of this sorts it — the account won\'t re-add, or mail fails everywhere including webmail — that\'s exactly what we\'re for. We can connect via <a href="/remote-support/">remote support</a>, often the same day, on a no-fix-no-fee basis. There\'s a broader library of fixes on our <a href="/outlook-problems/">Outlook problems</a> page, or call 01202 775566 and talk to a human in Bournemouth.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Why do my emails go to Drafts instead of sending in the new Outlook?', 'a': "Because new Outlook syncs Gmail, IMAP and most non-Microsoft accounts through Microsoft's cloud, and for many of these accounts there is no traditional Outbox — so when a send fails, the app parks the message back in Drafts, often without an error. Common triggers are a dropped connection, an expired sign-in, an oversized attachment or the mail server rejecting the message. The email isn't lost; it just hasn't gone. Open it and press Send again, and if it keeps happening, check the account is syncing, try removing and re-adding it — or consider switching back to classic Outlook, which still has a proper Outbox. One honest caveat: classic Outlook needs a Microsoft 365 subscription or an Office licence that includes it — if new Outlook is all you have ever had, there may be nothing to go back to, and we can talk you through the options."}, {'q': 'Where is the Outbox in the new Outlook?', 'a': "For many account types there isn't one — you're not missing it. New Outlook handles Gmail, IMAP and other non-Microsoft accounts through Microsoft's cloud sync, and in most of these setups unsent mail never appears in an Outbox folder; a failed send simply returns the message to Drafts. Some Microsoft 365 and Outlook.com configurations do still show an Outbox, and the app changes between versions, so your copy may differ. If you rely on seeing exactly what hasn't been sent yet, that's one of the strongest practical reasons people give for returning to classic Outlook."}, {'q': "How do I resend an email that's stuck in Drafts in new Outlook?", 'a': "Open the message in your Drafts folder and press Send again — that genuinely is the fix in most one-off cases, because the original failure was usually a momentary sync hiccup. Before resending, quickly check your Sent folder and webmail to make sure the first attempt didn't actually go; you don't want the recipient getting it twice. If the resend fails too, strip any large attachment and try a plain version, check the account is signed in and syncing, and update the app from the Microsoft Store. Repeated failures point to an account or app fault rather than bad luck."}, {'q': 'Can I go back to the old Outlook if the new one keeps doing this?', 'a': "Yes — classic Outlook still exists and is still supported by Microsoft, so switching back is a perfectly reasonable choice rather than a defeat. Many people prefer it precisely because it has a visible Outbox and talks to your mail server directly, so a stuck email is obvious instead of hiding in Drafts. Depending on your setup there's a toggle in the app, or you may need to reinstall classic Outlook — the route differs between the free Mail-replacement version and Microsoft 365. Our step-by-step guide on the site walks through going back safely without losing any mail."}, {'q': "Will I lose the email if it's stuck in Drafts and won't send?", 'a': "No — a message parked in Drafts is saved, not lost, so there's no need to retype it. The frustration is the opposite problem: it's safe but silent, and nobody has received it. Copy the text somewhere safe if it's important, then work through the checks — resend it, remove any large attachment, confirm the account is syncing, and test from webmail. The one habit worth building while you're on new Outlook is glancing at Drafts at the end of each day, so an unsent message can't hide there for a week before anyone notices."}], 'crossLinksHtml': '<p><strong>Related guides:</strong> <a href="/outlook-stuck-in-outbox/">Emails stuck in the Outbox (classic Outlook)</a> · <a href="/outlook-not-sending-emails/">Outlook not sending emails</a> · <a href="/how-to-go-back-to-classic-outlook/">How to go back to classic Outlook</a> · <a href="/outlook-problems/">Outlook problems: the full guide</a> · <a href="/remote-support/">Remote support</a></p>', 'primaryCta': ['Get it fixed remotely', '/remote-support/'], 'secondaryCta': ['Call 01202 775566', 'tel:+441202775566'], 'schemaKind': 'service'}, {'slug': 'new-outlook-only-showing-recent-emails', 'title': 'New Outlook Only Showing Recent Emails? | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': "New Outlook only showing recent emails, not old ones? They're almost certainly safe on the server. How to prove it, fix the sync window, and when to migrate.", 'ogTitle': "New Outlook Only Showing Recent Emails? Your Old Mail Isn't Gone", 'crumbName': 'New Outlook missing old emails', 'eyebrow': '// OUTLOOK HELP', 'h1': "New Outlook only showing recent emails? Your old mail almost certainly isn't gone", 'lede': "You've switched to the new Outlook and years of email look like they've vanished — only recent messages are showing. Take a breath: in almost every case the old mail is safe on the server, and the app is simply showing you a recent slice of it. This page shows you how to prove your mail exists in two minutes, which setting controls what you see, and when it's really a job for a proper migration.", 'chips': ['No-fix-no-fee remote support', 'Trading since 1995', 'Rated 4.9 on Google'], 'ctaHead': 'Want us to prove your mail is safe?', 'ctaSub': "Remote-first, no-fix-no-fee help from a Bournemouth family firm — we'll check the server with you before touching anything. Call 01202 775566.", 'serviceName': 'Outlook missing old emails help', 'sections': [{'eyebrow': '/01 &mdash; START HERE', 'h2': "Years of email look gone? They almost certainly aren't", 'html': "<p>You've opened the new Outlook and years of email have apparently vanished — the inbox only goes back a few weeks or months, and folders that held a decade of correspondence look nearly empty. It's a horrible moment, so let's deal with the fear first: <strong>in almost every case, nothing has been deleted.</strong> Your old mail is still sitting in your mailbox on the server.</p><p>The new Outlook works differently from the Outlook many of us grew up with. Rather than downloading and keeping everything on your computer, it reads your mailbox from the server and keeps a recent slice of it locally for speed. When that slice is all you can see, it feels like data loss — but it's a display and sync issue, not a deletion.</p><p>On this page we'll show you how to prove your mail exists in about two minutes, where the setting that limits your view lives, why search can make things look worse than they are, and the one genuine exception — old archive files — where the right answer is a proper migration.</p>"}, {'eyebrow': '/02 &mdash; QUICK PROOF', 'h2': 'Two minutes to prove your mail still exists', 'html': "<p>Before touching any settings, get your evidence. Open a web browser and sign in to your email provider's webmail — for Microsoft 365 and Outlook.com addresses that means signing in at office.com or outlook.com; other providers (BT, Gmail, your own domain's host) have their own webmail pages. Webmail shows you the mailbox as it actually exists on the server, with no app in the way.</p><p>If your old email is all there in webmail — and it usually is — you can relax. Nothing is lost, and the rest of this page is about persuading the app on your computer to show what the server already holds.</p><p>If the old mail <em>isn't</em> in webmail either, don't panic yet: this usually means it never lived on the server in the first place. Years of email from an older setup — particularly POP accounts and Classic Outlook — often sit in a local archive file on the computer instead. Skip to the last section below, because that's a different job with a happy ending of its own.</p>"}, {'eyebrow': '/03 &mdash; THE SETTING', 'h2': 'The sync window: why the app only shows a recent slice', 'html': '<p>Mail apps limit how much they keep on the device to save space and stay fast. Somewhere in the new Outlook\'s settings — and in Classic Outlook\'s account settings — there\'s a control that decides how far back that local slice reaches. Look for wording along the lines of <strong>"download email for"</strong>, <strong>"days of email to save"</strong> or <strong>"sync email from"</strong>; Microsoft moves the exact names and locations around between updates, which is why we\'re describing the setting rather than promising a click-path that may be wrong by the time you read this.</p><p>Set it to the longest window available — ideally "all" — then be patient. A large mailbox can take a while to come down, especially on a slower connection, and folders may fill in gradually rather than all at once.</p><p>Two honest notes. First, even with a short window the app should fetch older mail from the server when you scroll or search — but this fails often enough that lengthening the setting is still worth doing. Second, if the app is signed in but simply refuses to sync, signing out and back in — or removing and re-adding the account — resets a stuck sync more often than it should need to.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/04 &mdash; SEARCH QUIRK', 'h2': "The mail is there — but search says it isn't", 'html': '<p>A common variation: the old messages exist, and you can even reach them by scrolling, but typing a name or subject into search returns nothing older than a few weeks. That\'s a search problem, not a missing-mail problem, and it deserves its own page — see <a href="/outlook-search-not-finding-old-emails/">Outlook search not finding old emails</a> for the full treatment.</p><p>The quick cross-checks: browse the folder sorted by date instead of searching, and try the same search in webmail. Webmail searches the server\'s full index, so if it finds the message and the app doesn\'t, you\'ve confirmed the mail is safe and the fault is purely in the app\'s search.</p><p>If you\'re hitting several of these annoyances at once — missing mail, broken search, calendar oddities — our <a href="/outlook-problems/">Outlook problems hub</a> gathers the common new-Outlook complaints in one place, because they often share a cause.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/05 &mdash; HONEST ADVICE', 'h2': "When it's not worth fighting — and what we'd do", 'html': '<p>First, the free outcome: if webmail shows everything and lengthening the sync window brings it back into the app, you\'re done. Don\'t pay anyone — including us — for that.</p><p>Where we\'d stop fighting: if your old email lives in PST archive files built up over years of Classic Outlook or an old POP account, the new Outlook is simply the wrong tool. Its support for opening old archives has been arriving slowly and unevenly, and we wouldn\'t gamble a decade of correspondence on it. <a href="/how-to-go-back-to-classic-outlook/">Going back to Classic Outlook</a> is free, legitimate, and opens those archives natively — and Microsoft has committed to supporting it for years to come.</p><p>The durable fix is a one-off migration: getting the archive off a single ageing hard drive and into a server-side mailbox or a properly backed-up store, so every app and phone can see it and one disk failure can\'t take it away. That\'s careful, slightly tedious work with real pitfalls — mailbox size limits, duplicates, corrupt archive files — and it\'s a job we do regularly as part of our <a href="/email-support/">email support</a>. Remote-first, no-fix-no-fee: if we can\'t sort it, you don\'t pay. And if this page hasn\'t sorted it, that\'s exactly what we\'re for — 01202 775566.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Has the new Outlook deleted my old emails?', 'a': "Almost certainly not. The new Outlook reads your mailbox from the server rather than storing everything on your computer, and it keeps only a recent window of messages locally — so older mail can drop out of view without going anywhere. The fastest proof is signing in to your provider's webmail in a browser, where you see the mailbox exactly as it exists on the server. The one genuine exception is mail that only ever lived in a local archive file (a PST) on an older setup — that was never on the server, so a server-first app can't show it, but it still exists on the computer's drive."}, {'q': 'Why does the new Outlook only show my recent emails and not old ones?', 'a': "Because it's designed to keep a recent slice of your mailbox on the device and fetch the rest from the server on demand. If a sync or offline setting is set to a short window, the mailbox is very large, or the sync has quietly stuck, older messages fail to appear. There's also a separate case: if years of your email live in PST archive files from Classic Outlook or an old POP account, the new app can't simply open those, so everything before the switch looks missing. Checking webmail tells you which situation you're in. Note that classic Outlook needs a Microsoft 365 subscription or an Office licence that includes it — if new Outlook is all you have ever had, there may be nothing to switch back to."}, {'q': 'How do I get the new Outlook to show all my old emails?', 'a': "Find the sync or offline setting — the wording is along the lines of 'download email for' or 'days of email to save' — and set it to the longest option available (currently up to 180 days in new Outlook — only classic Outlook has an &ldquo;All&rdquo; setting) available, ideally all. Exact menu names move between versions, so we describe the setting rather than promise a click-path. Then give a large mailbox time to download; folders can fill in gradually. If the mail shows in webmail but still won't appear in the app, signing out and back in, or removing and re-adding the account, often resets a stuck sync. If none of that works, it's worth a professional look."}, {'q': "Where are my old emails if the new Outlook can't see them?", 'a': "One of two places. Most commonly they're still in your mailbox on the server, which you can confirm by signing in to webmail in a browser — the app just isn't showing them. Otherwise they're in a local archive file (a PST) created by Classic Outlook, which is very common with older POP accounts that downloaded mail to the computer and removed it from the server. Those files sit on the computer's drive or an old backup, and the new Outlook can't reliably open them — so the mail looks gone when it's actually just in a format the new app doesn't read."}, {'q': 'Should I go back to Classic Outlook to see my old emails?', 'a': "If your old mail lives in PST archive files, yes — that's the pragmatic move. Classic Outlook opens those archives natively, switching back is free, and Microsoft has committed to supporting it for years to come, so it isn't a dead end. Longer term, the resilient answer is migrating that archive into a server-side mailbox or a properly backed-up store, so any app or phone can see it and a single hard-drive failure can't take a decade of email with it. That's a one-off job worth doing properly rather than fighting the new app indefinitely."}], 'crossLinksHtml': '<p><strong>Related guides:</strong> <a href="/outlook-problems/">Outlook problems hub</a> · <a href="/outlook-search-not-finding-old-emails/">Outlook search not finding old emails</a> · <a href="/how-to-go-back-to-classic-outlook/">Go back to Classic Outlook</a> · <a href="/email-support/">Email support from 365 Techies</a></p>', 'primaryCta': ['Get it fixed remotely', '/remote-support/'], 'secondaryCta': ['Call 01202 775566', 'tel:+441202775566'], 'schemaKind': 'service'}, {'slug': 'room-by-room-wifi-test', 'title': 'Room by Room WiFi Test: Survey Your Home | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'How to run a room by room WiFi test properly: wired baseline, same-minute readings, what the numbers mean — plus a free tool that star-rates every room.', 'ogTitle': 'Room by Room WiFi Test — How to Survey Your Home Properly', 'crumbName': 'Room-by-Room WiFi Test', 'eyebrow': '// FREE TOOL + HOW-TO GUIDE', 'h1': 'The room-by-room WiFi test: survey your home properly', 'lede': "A single speed test tells you about one spot — usually the one nearest the router, which was never the problem. A room-by-room WiFi test walks the whole house, measures each room the same way, and shows you exactly where the signal falls over. Here's how to run one properly, what the numbers mean, and the free tool we built that saves every room, star-rates them and hands you a branded PDF.", 'chips': ['Free tool — no sign-up', 'Family firm since 1995', 'Rated 4.9 on Google'], 'ctaHead': "Survey done and the map's still ugly?", 'ctaSub': "Send us the PDF and we'll take it from there — remote-first, no-fix-no-fee. Call 01202 775566.", 'serviceName': 'WiFi Survey & Home Network Help', 'sections': [{'eyebrow': '/01 &mdash; THE PROBLEM', 'h2': "One speed test can't see your whole house", 'html': '<p>"The WiFi\'s fine in the lounge but hopeless in the back bedroom" — that\'s the sentence we hear most, and a single speed test can\'t explain it. One test measures one spot at one moment, usually with you standing near the router where things were never bad. It says nothing about the kitchen behind the chimney breast or the office in the loft conversion.</p><p>A room-by-room WiFi test is different: same device, same test, every room, a few minutes apart. Done properly, it turns "the internet\'s rubbish" into a map — three rooms brilliant, two fine, one genuinely poor — and that map tells you whether the fix is free (move the router), cheap (shift where you sit) or worth real money (a proper mesh system).</p><p>This guide shows you how to run that survey so the results actually mean something, what the numbers tell you about each room, and how to use our <a href="/wifi-signal-test/">free room-by-room WiFi test tool</a>, which saves each room as you go, star-rates them and produces a PDF you can keep or send to us.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/02 &mdash; THE METHOD', 'h2': 'How to run a survey that means something', 'html': '<p>The difference between a useful survey and a misleading one is discipline, and there are only four rules:</p><ol><li><strong>Get a baseline first.</strong> If you can, run one test on a computer wired into the router with an Ethernet cable — that\'s what your broadband can actually do. No cable? Test on WiFi standing right next to the router instead. Every room gets judged against this ceiling, not against whatever speed the adverts promised.</li><li><strong>Same device, every room.</strong> Phones, laptops and tablets have different WiFi radios; mixing them ruins the comparison.</li><li><strong>Same few minutes.</strong> Broadband speed drifts through the day, so a survey spread across an afternoon compares apples with oranges. Walk the whole house in one 10–15 minute pass.</li><li><strong>Stand where you actually use it.</strong> Test from the desk chair, the sofa, the pillow end of the bed — not the doorway, which is often several steps closer to the router than you ever sit.</li></ol><p>Our <a href="/wifi-signal-test/">live WiFi test</a> is built around exactly this routine: start it, walk, hold still in each room while it takes a reading, name the room and save. It keeps every result and ranks your rooms from best to worst.</p><p>A caveat on the wired baseline: use a gigabit-capable cable and port. An old cable or a laptop with a 100 Mbps port caps a wired test at about 94 Mbps — on full fibre that makes the whole survey look wrong. If a wired test on a fast package sits suspiciously close to 94, suspect the cable before the broadband.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/03 &mdash; READING THE RESULTS', 'h2': 'What the numbers mean, room by room', 'html': "<p>Three numbers tell the story in each room, and raw speed is the least interesting of them:</p><ul><li><strong>Download</strong> is how fast things arrive. Streaming and big downloads care about this most.</li><li><strong>Latency</strong> is the round-trip delay. Video calls and gaming feel this long before they feel a slow download.</li><li><strong>Jitter</strong> is how much that delay wobbles. It's the number behind frozen faces and robot voices on calls.</li></ul><p>This is why the video-call bar matters more than the headline speed. A modern video call needs surprisingly little bandwidth — a few steady Mbps is generally enough for HD, though the exact figure varies by app — but it needs latency that stays put. A bedroom showing 40 Mbps with wildly swinging jitter will drop more calls than a box room showing 10 Mbps rock-steady.</p><p>One honest note about signal bars: no website can read your actual radio signal strength (the dBm figure) or your network's name — browsers simply aren't allowed to see them, and any page claiming otherwise is guessing. So instead of pretending to read the radio, our tool measures what you actually experience — real download, real latency, real jitter, live — and turns them into a plain-English star rating for each room.</p>"}, {'eyebrow': '/04 &mdash; UK HOMES', 'h2': 'Why the back bedroom is always the worst room', 'html': '<p>UK housing is unusually hostile to WiFi, and your survey will usually show it. Solid brick and stone internal walls — normal in anything pre-war — soak up far more signal than the timber-and-plasterboard construction much WiFi kit is designed around. Chimney breasts are effectively signal walls. Modern extensions and loft conversions often use foil-backed insulation boards, which are lovely for warmth and terrible for WiFi. We\'ve written up the full picture in our guide to <a href="/wifi-uk-buildings-heat/">why UK buildings are so hard on WiFi</a>.</p><p>Add the classic layout problem — the master socket, and therefore the router, sits by the front door while the office is at the back — and a two-star back bedroom is the predictable result, not a faulty router.</p><p>Your room map tells you which cure fits. One poor room next to good ones can often be fixed by moving the router or simply shifting where you sit. Several poor rooms strung out across solid walls or multiple floors is the honest case for a <a href="/mesh-wifi-systems-uk/">mesh WiFi system</a> — we\'ve compared the ones that actually suit UK homes, and the survey PDF is exactly the evidence that decision should rest on.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/05 &mdash; HONEST ADVICE', 'h2': "When it's not worth fighting it — and what we'd do", 'html': '<p>Sometimes the survey\'s most valuable answer is "stop — don\'t spend anything." Three results should put your wallet away:</p><ul><li><strong>The wired baseline itself is poor.</strong> If the connection is slow with a cable plugged straight into the router, no WiFi equipment on earth will fix it — the problem is the broadband coming into the house, and that\'s a conversation with your provider about the line or the package.</li><li><strong>Every room scores close to the baseline.</strong> Your WiFi is already doing its job. If things still feel slow, the fault lies elsewhere — start with our <a href="/wifi-troubleshooting/">WiFi troubleshooting guide</a> before buying anything.</li><li><strong>One bad room, everything else fine.</strong> Try the free fixes first: get the router up off the floor, out of the cupboard and away from the TV, then re-run the survey. A surprising number of "we need mesh" houses don\'t.</li></ul><p>And if the map is genuinely ugly — or you\'d rather not fight it — that\'s exactly what we\'re for. Download the PDF from the <a href="/wifi-signal-test/">test tool</a>, call us on 01202 775566, and we\'ll go through it with you. Remote-first, no-fix-no-fee, and we\'ll tell you honestly if the cheapest fix is buying nothing at all.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Why is my WiFi good in one room and bad in another?', 'a': 'Because WiFi is radio, and every wall, floor and chimney breast between your router and that room soaks up part of the signal. UK homes are particularly tough: solid brick or stone internal walls absorb far more than plasterboard, and foil-backed insulation in extensions and loft conversions can block signal almost entirely. Distance matters too, and the faster 5 GHz band travels through walls worse than the older 2.4 GHz band. A room-by-room test shows you exactly how much each room loses compared with standing next to the router — which is what tells you whether the fix is free or needs kit.'}, {'q': 'How do I test my WiFi signal in every room of my house?', 'a': 'Run the same test, on the same device, in every room, within the same ten to fifteen minutes — and take a baseline first, wired into the router if possible, so each room has something fair to be compared against. Stand where you actually use the internet in each room, not in the doorway, and hold still while the reading runs. Our free room-by-room WiFi test does the legwork: it measures live as you walk, saves a named reading for each room, star-rates them all and gives you a branded PDF of the results at the end.'}, {'q': 'What speed do I need for video calls like Zoom or Teams?', 'a': "Less than most people think — a few steady Mbps is generally enough for an HD call, though the exact requirement varies by app and settings. What kills video calls usually isn't speed but stability: latency (the delay) and jitter (how much that delay wobbles). A room with modest download but rock-steady latency will hold a call better than a fast room with spiky jitter. That's why our test measures all three in every room and rates each room specifically for video calls, rather than just showing a headline speed."}, {'q': 'Can a website really measure my WiFi signal strength?', 'a': 'Not in the radio sense — no website can read your actual signal strength in dBm or see your network\'s name, because browsers deliberately block access to that information. Any web page showing you "signal bars" is guessing. What a browser can measure honestly is your real connection quality: actual download speed, latency and jitter, sampled live as you move around the house. That\'s what our tool does — it tells you how each room genuinely performs rather than pretending to read the radio. For a true dBm survey you need an app installed on the device, or an engineer\'s kit.'}, {'q': 'Do I need a mesh WiFi system if one room has bad WiFi?', 'a': 'Usually not for a single room — try the free fixes first. Moving the router up off the floor, out of the cupboard and away from the TV often rescues one weak room, and re-running the survey afterwards takes two minutes. Mesh earns its money when several rooms test poorly across solid walls or multiple floors, which is exactly the pattern a room-by-room map reveals. If your survey shows that, our UK mesh guide compares systems that cope with brick walls; if it shows one marginal room, save your money.'}], 'crossLinksHtml': '<p><strong>Keep going:</strong> <a href="/wifi-signal-test/">Free room-by-room WiFi test tool</a> · <a href="/wifi-troubleshooting/">WiFi troubleshooting guide</a> · <a href="/wifi-uk-buildings-heat/">Why UK buildings are hard on WiFi</a> · <a href="/mesh-wifi-systems-uk/">Mesh WiFi systems for UK homes</a></p>', 'primaryCta': ['Run the free WiFi survey', '/wifi-signal-test/'], 'secondaryCta': ['Call 01202 775566', 'tel:+441202775566'], 'schemaKind': 'service'}, {'slug': 'wifi-heatmap-online', 'title': 'WiFi Heatmap Online — Free, No Download? | 365 Techies', 'metaDesc': 'The honest answer to "wifi heatmap online free no download": browsers can\'t read signal strength. See what you CAN map from a browser — and when to go pro.', 'ogTitle': 'WiFi Heatmap Online (Free, No Download): The Honest Answer', 'crumbName': 'WiFi Heatmap Online', 'eyebrow': '// FREE WIFI TOOLS — HONEST EDITION', 'h1': 'WiFi heatmap online, free, no download — the honest answer', 'lede': "You want to see where your WiFi is strong and weak without installing anything. Fair enough — but no website can genuinely do that, because browsers can't read signal strength. Rather than pretend otherwise, this page explains what a real heatmap needs, what your browser can honestly measure room by room (often the more useful map), and when a professional survey is — and isn't — worth paying for.", 'chips': ['Trading since 1995', '4.9-rated on Google', 'No-fix-no-fee support'], 'ctaHead': 'Want it sorted rather than measured?', 'ctaSub': 'Remote-first WiFi help with no-fix-no-fee, free local collection if hardware is involved, and honest advice about the cheapest fix first. Call 01202 775566.', 'serviceName': 'WiFi Troubleshooting &amp; Surveys', 'sections': [{'eyebrow': '/01 &mdash; THE STRAIGHT ANSWER', 'h2': "No browser can make a true WiFi heatmap — here's why, and what to do instead", 'html': '<p>You typed something like "wifi heatmap online free no download" hoping a website could scan your house and paint a coloured map of where the signal is strong and weak. We\'ll be straight with you, because most pages ranking for this search aren\'t: <strong>no website can do that</strong>. A real heatmap plots radio signal strength — measured in dBm — at dozens of points on your floor plan, and web browsers are deliberately locked out of your WiFi radio. There is no browser feature that lets a page read signal strength, see nearby networks, or even know which access point you\'re connected to; it would be a privacy disaster if there were.</p><p>So any site promising a heatmap "online, no download" is either guessing from a speed test or drawing pretty pictures. But your search isn\'t a dead end. Below: what a genuine heatmap actually requires, the genuinely useful thing a browser <em>can</em> measure room by room — which for most homes is the better answer anyway — and our honest take on when to spend nothing at all, and when it\'s worth calling someone like us.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/02 &mdash; HOW REAL HEATMAPS WORK', 'h2': 'A true heatmap needs installed software reading your WiFi card', 'html': "<p>A genuine WiFi heatmap works like this: you install an app on a laptop — NetSpot and Ekahau are the best-known names in this class — load or sketch your floor plan, then walk the building clicking your position as you go. The app reads the signal strength your WiFi adapter is actually receiving at each point, then fills in the gaps to produce those familiar red-to-green colour maps. The measurement is the whole product, and it can only come from software with direct access to the wireless hardware — exactly what a browser is forbidden from having.</p><p>Free options do exist, with caveats. Some heatmap apps offer a free tier for home use, though what's included changes over time, so check the current limits rather than taking an old article's word for it. On Android, free WiFi analyser apps can show live signal strength as you walk, which makes a rough-and-ready survey; Apple restricts this on iPhone, so an iPhone alone can't do the job. And if you just want one honest number with nothing new installed, Windows already has it: open a command prompt, type <strong>netsh wlan show interfaces</strong>, and look at the Signal line for your current connection.</p>"}, {'eyebrow': '/03 &mdash; WHAT A BROWSER CAN DO', 'h2': 'What a browser can measure — often the more useful map', 'html': '<p>Here\'s the part most pages skip: signal strength was only ever a proxy for the thing you actually care about. Nobody\'s real question is "what dBm am I getting in the back bedroom?" It\'s "why do video calls break up in the back bedroom?" — and a browser <em>can</em> measure that directly. From a web page we can time how long data takes to travel (latency), how much that varies (jitter, the thing that wrecks calls), whether packets go missing, and how fast the connection really runs — separately, in every room you stand in.</p><p>Do that in each room and you\'ve built a connection-quality map: not a coloured floor plan, but an honest picture of where your WiFi works and where it doesn\'t. Often it\'s more useful than a heatmap, because it catches problems signal strength misses — a room can show full bars and still stutter thanks to interference or congestion, which is common in UK brick and stone homes where <a href="/wifi-uk-buildings-heat/">the building itself shapes the signal</a>. Our free <a href="/wifi-signal-test/">WiFi signal test</a> measures exactly these things from your browser — nothing to install, nothing to sign up for.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/04 &mdash; DIY IN TEN MINUTES', 'h2': 'Build your own connection-quality map in about ten minutes', 'html': '<p>The DIY version takes about ten minutes with a laptop or phone and our free <a href="/room-by-room-wifi-test/">room-by-room WiFi test</a>. Stand where you actually use the internet — sofa, desk, kitchen table, the bedroom where films buffer — run the test in each spot, and save a reading for every room as you go — our tool scores each room, star-rates it and builds a report you can download. At the end you have a room-by-room comparison rather than a vague sense that "upstairs is worse".</p><p>Then read it the way an engineer would:</p><ul><li><strong>Speed falls off a cliff in certain rooms</strong> — distance and walls; classic signal decay. Router placement or a mesh node is the likely fix.</li><li><strong>Speed is fine but jitter or packet loss is high</strong> — interference or a congested band; moving the router won\'t help much, changing channel or band might.</li><li><strong>One device struggles where others are fine</strong> — suspect the device, not the WiFi.</li></ul><p>Settings screens move between routers and Windows versions, so we won\'t pretend to give you exact click-paths — but the logic holds everywhere: measure where you actually live, compare, and the weak spot names itself.</p>'}, {'eyebrow': '/05 &mdash; OUR HONEST ADVICE', 'h2': "When it's not worth fighting it — and what we'd actually do", 'html': '<p>Our honest advice, as people who fix this for a living: for a normal home, don\'t buy survey software, and think twice before spending an afternoon building a heatmap at all. Most domestic WiFi problems come down to a router in a terrible spot (cupboards and floor level are the usual suspects), one dead zone that a single mesh node cures, or old kit that was never going to cover a three-storey house. A ten-minute room-by-room test finds those. A heatmap finds the same thing, prettier and slower.</p><p>Where we <em>would</em> use proper survey kit — and do — is where the building or the stakes are bigger: offices with several access points, thick-walled or steel-framed buildings, sites where a dropped connection costs money, or anywhere about to spend properly on new networking and wanting it placed right first time. That\'s what our <a href="/business-wifi-installation/">business WiFi installation and survey service</a> is for — we measure on site with real tools and design around what the building actually does to the signal.</p><p>And if you\'ve run the tests and the answer still isn\'t obvious? That\'s exactly what we\'re for. Remote-first, no-fix-no-fee, and we\'ll tell you honestly if the free fix is the right one. Call 01202 775566.</p>'}], 'faqs': [{'q': 'Can I make a WiFi heatmap online without downloading anything?', 'a': 'No — a real heatmap needs installed software that reads signal strength from your WiFi adapter, and web browsers are not allowed to do that. Any site offering an "online heatmap" is estimating from speed tests at best. What a browser genuinely can do is measure the quality of your connection — speed, delay, stability — from each room, which for most people answers the real question ("why is WiFi bad in that room?") just as well. Our free room-by-room test does exactly that, with nothing to install and nothing to sign up for.'}, {'q': "Why can't a website measure my WiFi signal strength?", 'a': "Because browsers deliberately have no access to your WiFi radio. There is no web feature that exposes signal strength, nearby network names, or which access point you're connected to — and for good reason: websites being able to scan the networks around you would be a serious privacy problem, since nearby network names can reveal where you are. This is a rule of how the web works, not a limitation of any one tool, so it applies equally to every site claiming to offer an online heatmap."}, {'q': 'Is there a genuinely free WiFi heatmap app?', 'a': "Yes — free heatmap apps exist for laptops, in the NetSpot class, though free tiers are limited and the limits change over time, so check before relying on one. You install the app, load or sketch a floor plan, and walk the house clicking your position while it reads signal strength. On Android, free WiFi analyser apps can show live signal as you walk; Apple restricts this on iPhone, so an iPhone alone can't do a proper survey. If that sounds like more effort than the problem deserves, it usually is — a room-by-room quality test is faster."}, {'q': 'Do I actually need a heatmap to fix bad WiFi?', 'a': "Usually not. A heatmap tells you where signal is weak, which you mostly already know — it's the room you searched from. What actually fixes WiFi is acting on that: moving the router somewhere sensible, adding a mesh node halfway to the dead zone, or shifting a stuttering device to a less congested band. A simple room-by-room test that measures real performance usually points at the same fix a heatmap would, in a tenth of the time. Heatmaps earn their keep in larger or trickier buildings — and that's professional survey territory."}, {'q': 'When is a professional WiFi survey worth paying for?', 'a': "When the building or the stakes outgrow DIY: business premises with multiple access points, thick brick or steel-framed buildings, warehouses, surgeries or offices where dropped connections cost money, or anywhere about to spend serious money on new kit that needs placing right first time. A professional survey uses proper equipment to map signal and interference on site. For a normal home, we'd honestly steer you away from paying for one — a free room-by-room test and a well-placed mesh node solve most houses. We cover Bournemouth, Poole and the surrounding area."}], 'crossLinksHtml': '<p><strong>Keep going:</strong> <a href="/room-by-room-wifi-test/">Room-by-room WiFi test (free, in your browser)</a> · <a href="/wifi-signal-test/">WiFi signal test</a> · <a href="/wifi-uk-buildings-heat/">Why UK buildings are hard on WiFi</a> · <a href="/business-wifi-installation/">Business WiFi installation &amp; surveys</a></p>', 'primaryCta': ['Run the free WiFi survey', '/wifi-signal-test/'], 'secondaryCta': ['Call 01202 775566', 'tel:+441202775566'], 'schemaKind': 'service'}]
